<h4>Why 72 hours</h4>
<p>Help may take three days or more after a big storm, quake or outage. A kit covers the gap. Keep it in one place, in a bag or bin you can carry.</p>
<h4>Water and food (per person)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Water:</strong> 4 L (1 gallon) per day, so 12 L for three days. Add purification tablets or a filter.</li>
<li>Food that needs no cooking: canned goods with a manual opener, protein bars, nuts, dried fruit, crackers, peanut butter.</li>
<li>Baby food, formula and pet food if needed.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Light, power and communication</h4>
<ul>
<li>Flashlight or headlamp and spare batteries</li>
<li>Battery or hand-crank radio (NOAA weather radio if you can)</li>
<li><strong>Power bank, charging cables and this device</strong></li>
<li>Whistle, and a printed list of key phone numbers</li>
<li>Cash in small bills</li>
</ul>
<h4>First aid and health</h4>
<ul>
<li>First aid kit, any prescriptions for a week or more, glasses, a copy of the medication list</li>
<li>Pain reliever, antihistamine, anti-diarrheal, sunscreen, insect repellent</li>
<li>Masks, gloves and hand sanitizer</li>
</ul>
<h4>Shelter and warmth</h4>
<ul>
<li>Warm clothes, a change of clothes, sturdy shoes, rain gear</li>
<li>Emergency blankets or sleeping bags, a tarp, and rope</li>
<li>Matches or lighters in a waterproof bag</li>
</ul>
<h4>Tools and hygiene</h4>
<ul>
<li>Multi-tool or knife, duct tape, work gloves</li>
<li>Trash bags, toilet paper, soap, wipes, feminine products, toothbrush</li>
<li>Garbage bags and a bucket for an emergency toilet</li>
</ul>
<h4>Papers</h4>
<ul>
<li>Copies of ID, insurance, deeds and bank info in a waterproof bag, plus a USB copy.</li>
<li>Local maps and your family plan.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Keep it fresh</h4>
<ul>
<li>Check the kit <strong>twice a year</strong>. Swap food, water, batteries and medicines before they expire.</li>
<li>Update sizes of kids' clothes.</li>
<li>Keep a smaller kit in each car.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Start with water, food, light and a radio. You can add the rest over time.</blockquote><p>A small kit you actually carry beats a big kit left at home. Build it around the Rule of 3s.</p>
<h4>Core items</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fire:</strong> lighter, ferro rod, waterproof matches, tinder (cotton balls with petroleum jelly)</li>
<li><strong>Shelter:</strong> emergency blanket or bivy bag, small tarp, 15 m of cord</li>
<li><strong>Water:</strong> metal bottle you can boil in, purification tablets, filter</li>
<li><strong>Light:</strong> headlamp with spare batteries</li>
<li><strong>Signal:</strong> whistle, signal mirror, bright cloth</li>
<li><strong>Tools:</strong> fixed-blade or folding knife, duct tape</li>
<li><strong>First aid:</strong> bandages, gauze, tape, tourniquet, pain relievers, personal medicines</li>
<li><strong>Navigation:</strong> map of the area, compass</li>
<li><strong>Food:</strong> high-calorie bars</li>
</ul>
<h4>Clothing</h4>
<ul>
<li>Warm layer (wool or synthetic, not cotton)</li>
<li>Rain jacket</li>
<li>Hat and gloves</li>
<li>Spare socks</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Cotton loses its warmth when wet. In cold or wet weather, wool and synthetics can be the difference between cold and hypothermic.</blockquote>
<h4>Tell someone</h4>
<p>The most important item is not in the kit: leave your route and expected return time with someone you trust.</p><p>Most winter storm deaths come from car trouble, exposure, carbon monoxide and heart strain from shoveling.</p>
<h4>Before the storm</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stock food, water, blankets, batteries and any medicines you need. See <a class="xl" data-id="Home Emergencies and Power Outages">Home Emergencies and Power Outages</a>.</li>
<li>Fill the car with fuel and keep a winter kit in it. See <a class="xl" data-id="Car Emergency Kit">Car Emergency Kit</a>.</li>
<li>Plan how you will keep one room warm if the power fails.</li>
</ul>
<h4>During a blizzard</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stay inside and off the roads.</strong> Whiteouts make it hard to see even a few feet.</li>
<li>Heat one room. Close doors, stuff towels under them and hang blankets over windows.</li>
<li>Dress in layers and keep a hat on. See <a class="xl" data-id="Cold Weather and Snow Shelters">Cold Weather and Snow Shelters</a>.</li>
<li>Keep fluids up. Eat regularly, because your body burns food to make heat.</li>
<li><strong>Never burn charcoal or run a generator or camp stove indoors.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Carbon Monoxide">Carbon Monoxide</a>.</li>
<li>Use flashlights, not candles. If you must use a heater, keep it away from things that burn.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Ice storms</h4>
<ul>
<li>Ice downs trees and power lines. Stay away from windows, trees and lines.</li>
<li>Walk with short, flat steps, feet turned out slightly, arms free.</li>
<li>Do not drive on ice. Even 4-wheel drive does not help you stop.</li>
<li>Use handrails where there are any, and carry a small bag of sand or cat litter to sprinkle on ice.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Shoveling</h4>
<ul>
<li>Heavy snow causes heart attacks. Push snow rather than lifting it, take breaks, and stop for chest pain, dizziness or shortness of breath.</li>
<li>Keep vents for furnaces, dryers and stoves clear of snow so exhaust can escape.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Frostbite and hypothermia</h4>
<p>Watch for numb, white or waxy skin, and shivering, slurred speech or confusion. See <a class="xl" data-id="Hypothermia and Frostbite">Hypothermia and Frostbite</a>.</p><p>Standing near moving traffic is one of the most dangerous places to be. Think about traffic first.</p>
<h4>If the car is slowing down or dying</h4>
<ul>
<li>Turn on the hazard lights right away.</li>
<li>Steer toward the <strong>right shoulder</strong> or an exit, a parking lot or any wide, flat spot well off the road. Coast as far as you can.</li>
<li>Do not slam on the brakes. Brake gently.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Once you have stopped</h4>
<ul>
<li>Turn the wheels away from the road. Set the parking brake and put the car in Park.</li>
<li>Keep the hazard lights on. Raise the hood or tie a white cloth to the door handle or antenna.</li>
<li><strong>Call for help.</strong> Say where you are: road name, direction, mile marker or the last exit.</li>
<li>If you are on a busy highway with no barrier and cannot reach safety, <strong>stay in the car with your seat belt on</strong> and call for help. Do not stand behind or beside the car.</li>
<li>If you can get to safety, <strong>get out on the side away from traffic</strong> and move well away from the road, behind a guardrail or up a bank.</li>
<li>Do not try to repair the car with traffic passing close by.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Safety</h4>
<ul>
<li>Be careful with strangers. Stay in your locked car if someone stops, speak through a cracked window and ask them to call for help. Do not accept a ride if you do not have to.</li>
<li>At night, turn on the dome light so you can be seen and avoid sitting in the dark.</li>
<li>If you are not sure where you are, keep your phone charged. See <a class="xl" data-id="Radio and Phone Tips">Radio and Phone Tips</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Simple things to check</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Out of fuel:</strong> you need a can of fuel or a tow.</li>
<li><strong>Won't start, clicks:</strong> probably a weak battery. See <a class="xl" data-id="Jump-Starting a Car">Jump-Starting a Car</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Overheating:</strong> pull over, turn off the engine and <strong>do not open the radiator cap</strong> while it is hot. Wait at least 30 minutes.</li>
<li><strong>Flat tire:</strong> see <a class="xl" data-id="Changing a Flat Tire">Changing a Flat Tire</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Smoke from the hood:</strong> see <a class="xl" data-id="Vehicle Fire">Vehicle Fire</a>.</li>
</ul><h4>Signs of a break</h4>
<p>Pain, swelling, bruising, deformity, grating, or the person cannot use the limb. If in doubt, treat it as broken.</p>
<h4>Splinting</h4>
<ol>
<li>Control any bleeding first. Cover open wounds and bone ends with a clean dressing. Do not push bone back in.</li>
<li><strong>Check circulation, feeling and movement</strong> beyond the injury (fingers or toes): warm, pink, can feel your touch, can wiggle.</li>
<li>Splint the limb in the position you find it, unless it is badly bent and there is no pulse beyond it (then gently straighten it once, along its length, if trained).</li>
<li>Immobilize the <strong>joint above and below</strong> a broken bone, or the bones above and below an injured joint.</li>
<li>Use anything rigid: sticks, trekking poles, rolled magazines, a sleeping pad. Pad well with clothing.</li>
<li>Tie in place firmly but not tightly, with knots away from the injury.</li>
<li><strong>Re-check circulation</strong> after splinting and every 30 minutes. If fingers or toes go pale, cold or numb, loosen the ties.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Slings</h4>
<p>For arm, wrist and collarbone injuries, support the arm in a sling made from a triangle of cloth or a shirt, with the hand slightly higher than the elbow.</p>
<h4>Sprains</h4>
<p>Rest, cool packs wrapped in cloth for 20 minutes at a time, gentle compression wrap, and raise the limb. A sprained ankle may be walkable with a firm wrap and a stick if you must move.</p>
<blockquote>Suspect a <strong>spine injury</strong> after a fall, crash or blow to the head with neck or back pain, numbness or tingling. Keep the head and neck still and do not move the person unless they are in danger.</blockquote><h4>Stop the burning</h4>
<ul>
<li>If clothing is on fire: stop, drop and roll, or smother with a blanket.</li>
<li>Remove rings, watches and tight clothing near the burn before it swells. Do not pull off clothing stuck to the skin.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cool it</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cool with clean, cool running water for <strong>20 minutes</strong> (at least 10). This helps even if started late, up to about 3 hours after.</li>
<li>Do <strong>not</strong> use ice, butter, oil, toothpaste or ointment on a fresh burn.</li>
<li>Keep the rest of the person warm. Cooling a large burn can cause hypothermia, especially in children.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cover it</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cover loosely with clean plastic wrap (laid on, not wrapped tight), a clean non-stick dressing, or a clean plastic bag over a hand.</li>
<li>Do not pop blisters.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Serious burns - get help</h4>
<ul>
<li>Larger than the person's palm, or deep (white, leathery or charred)</li>
<li>On the face, hands, feet, genitals or over joints</li>
<li>Electrical or chemical burns</li>
<li>Burns in the airway: soot around the nose or mouth, hoarse voice, coughing, trouble breathing. These can swell shut. <strong>Urgent.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h4>Chemical burns</h4>
<p>Brush off dry chemicals, then flush with lots of water for at least 20 minutes.</p>
<blockquote>Large burns cause dangerous fluid loss. Watch for <a class="xl" data-id="Shock">Shock</a> and give sips of fluid if the person is alert and help is far away.</blockquote><p>A burst pipe can pour out hundreds of liters of water an hour and ruin a house in a day.</p>
<h4>If a pipe bursts</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Shut off the main water valve</strong> right away. See <a class="xl" data-id="Utility Shutoffs">Utility Shutoffs</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Open faucets</strong> (cold and hot) at the lowest and highest points to drain what is left in the pipes.</li>
<li><strong>Turn off the electricity</strong> to wet areas if you can do it safely from a dry spot at the panel. <strong>Do not step into standing water near outlets or appliances.</strong></li>
<li>Turn off the water heater (power or gas) so it does not run dry and burn out.</li>
<li>Move belongings out of the water and mop up. Use fans and open windows to dry the area quickly.</li>
<li>Take photos for insurance, and call a plumber.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Frozen pipes (water not coming out)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Open the faucet so melting water can flow.</li>
<li>Warm the pipe slowly with a <strong>hair dryer</strong>, a heating pad or warm towels. Start near the faucet and work back.</li>
<li><strong>Never use an open flame, a propane torch or a kerosene heater.</strong> They can start a fire or burst the pipe.</li>
<li>If you cannot find the frozen section, or the pipe is already cracked, shut off the water and call a plumber.</li>
<li>Keep the faucet open until full flow returns.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prevent freezing</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep the house at no lower than 13 C (55 F) even when away.</li>
<li>In a hard freeze, let a faucet <strong>drip</strong> and open cabinet doors under sinks.</li>
<li>Insulate pipes in garages, crawl spaces and outside walls.</li>
<li>Disconnect and drain outdoor hoses and shut off outdoor spigots in fall.</li>
<li>If the power may fail for long, drain the pipes: shut off the main, open all faucets and flush toilets.</li>
</ul>
<h4>After</h4>
<p>Water-damaged materials can grow mold in 24 to 48 hours. Dry things out fast and throw out soaked insulation and carpets.</p><p>Build a kit once, keep it in the trunk, and check it twice a year.</p>
<h4>All year</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>First aid kit</strong> and any needed medicines. See <a class="xl" data-id="Basic Survival Kit">Basic Survival Kit</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Water</strong> (at least a liter or quart per person) and non-perishable snacks.</li>
<li><strong>Flashlight</strong> or headlamp with spare batteries, and a whistle.</li>
<li><strong>Phone charger</strong> or power bank, and a paper map. See <a class="xl" data-id="Map and Compass Basics">Map and Compass Basics</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Jumper cables or a jump pack.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Jump-Starting a Car">Jump-Starting a Car</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Spare tire</strong> (check the pressure), jack, lug wrench. See <a class="xl" data-id="Changing a Flat Tire">Changing a Flat Tire</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Tire plug kit or sealant</strong> and a small air pump that plugs into the car.</li>
<li><strong>Window breaker and seat belt cutter.</strong> Keep it within reach. See <a class="xl" data-id="Car in Water">Car in Water</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Warning triangles or reflective flares</strong>, and a high-visibility vest.</li>
<li><strong>Small fire extinguisher</strong>, mounted inside the cabin.</li>
<li>Duct tape, a multi-tool, gloves, rags, zip ties and rope.</li>
<li>Paper towels and a little cash in small bills.</li>
<li>Pen and paper, and a list of emergency numbers.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Add for winter</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blankets or a sleeping bag</strong>, warm coat, hat, gloves and boots.</li>
<li><strong>Ice scraper and brush</strong>, a small shovel and a bag of sand or cat litter for traction.</li>
<li>Hand warmers.</li>
<li>Do not burn candles or stoves inside a closed car. Use the engine for heat and clothes for warmth. See <a class="xl" data-id="Stranded in Your Car in Winter">Stranded in Your Car in Winter</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Extra food and water.</strong> A frozen bottle is useless; keep it in the cabin.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Keep the car ready</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep the fuel tank at least half full.</li>
<li>Check tires, battery, lights, wipers and fluids before long trips and before winter.</li>
<li>Tell someone your route and when you will arrive. See <a class="xl" data-id="Travel and Staying Found">Travel and Staying Found</a>.</li>
</ul><p>A car in deep water usually floats for 30 to 60 seconds before it sinks. <strong>Act at once.</strong> Do not call for help first and do not wait for the car to fill with water.</p>
<h4>Remember: Seat belt, Window, Children, Out</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Seat belt:</strong> unbuckle yourself.</li>
<li><strong>Window:</strong> open or break a <strong>side window</strong> now. Electric windows often still work for the first moments. If not, use a <strong>window-breaker tool</strong> (hit it at a corner of the glass, not the center). Windshields are hard to break.</li>
<li><strong>Children:</strong> unbuckle the oldest children first so they can help. Get the youngest out next. Hold on to them.</li>
<li><strong>Out:</strong> climb out of the window, head first or feet first, and swim away from the car to safety.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Important</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>The door will not open</strong> until the water pressure inside and outside is nearly equal. Do not waste time on it.</li>
<li>If you cannot get a window open, stay calm and move to the highest air pocket. When the car is nearly full of water the pressure equalizes and the door can open. Take a breath, open the door and swim out.</li>
<li>Keep calm. Focus on one step at a time.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are caught in floodwater</h4>
<p>Leave the vehicle before the water gets high. See <a class="xl" data-id="Floods and Flash Floods">Floods and Flash Floods</a>.</p>
<h4>Prevent it</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Never drive into water over a road.</strong> Turn around.</li>
<li>Keep a window-breaker and seat belt cutter within reach, such as on the sun visor or key ring. Not in the glove box.</li>
<li>Learn the steps with your family.</li>
</ul>
<p>After you are out, get warm and dry and watch for hypothermia. See <a class="xl" data-id="Hypothermia and Frostbite">Hypothermia and Frostbite</a>.</p><p>Carbon monoxide (CO) has no smell, color or taste. It comes from anything that burns fuel: furnaces, water heaters, stoves, fireplaces, gas heaters, generators, grills and cars. It builds up in closed spaces and can kill in minutes at high levels.</p>
<h4>Signs</h4>
<ul>
<li>Headache (the most common), dizziness, weakness</li>
<li>Nausea or vomiting, confusion</li>
<li>Chest pain, blurred vision</li>
<li><strong>Several people or pets sick at once</strong>, or symptoms that get better when you leave the building.</li>
<li>People who are asleep can die without any warning.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If the alarm sounds or you suspect CO</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get everyone out into fresh air at once</strong>, including pets. Do not stop to open windows.</li>
<li><strong>Call 911</strong> (or your local emergency number) from outside.</li>
<li>Count everyone. Do not go back in until firefighters say it is safe.</li>
<li>Get checked at a hospital even if you feel better. Tell them you may have had CO exposure.</li>
</ol>
<h4>First aid</h4>
<ul>
<li>Move the person to fresh air and keep them calm and warm.</li>
<li>If they are not breathing, start CPR. See <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a>.</li>
<li>A person who is unconscious, confused, pregnant, or a child needs urgent hospital care. They may need oxygen.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prevention</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Put battery or plug-in CO alarms on every level and near sleeping areas.</strong> Test them monthly and replace them when they expire (about every 5 to 7 years).</li>
<li><strong>Never run a generator, grill, camp stove or heater inside</strong> a house, garage, tent, camper or near an open window. See <a class="xl" data-id="Generator Safety">Generator Safety</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Never run a car in an attached garage</strong>, even with the door open.</li>
<li>Have furnaces, chimneys and water heaters checked each year.</li>
<li>After a storm or in winter, keep the tailpipe and exhaust vents clear of snow. See <a class="xl" data-id="Stranded in Your Car in Winter">Stranded in Your Car in Winter</a>.</li>
</ul><p>Babies depend on you for everything. These basics stay the same when the power is out.</p>
<h4>Feeding</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Breastfeeding is the safest and easiest</strong> in a disaster. It needs no water, no equipment and no power. Feed on demand and drink plenty yourself.</li>
<li><strong>Formula:</strong> ready-to-feed liquid formula is the safest, because it needs no water. Powder is the next choice.</li>
<li>Mix powder <strong>exactly</strong> as the label says. Never add extra water to stretch it, and never add extra powder. Wrong mixes can harm a baby.</li>
<li>Use <strong>safe water</strong>: bottled water, or water you have boiled for 1 minute and let cool. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making Water Safe to Drink">Making Water Safe to Drink</a>.</li>
<li>Throw away made-up formula that has been out more than 1 hour, or leftover from a feed.</li>
<li><strong>No plain water, juice, tea or honey</strong> for babies under 6 months. No honey under 1 year.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cleaning bottles</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wash with soap and safe water and rinse. Then <strong>sanitize</strong> by boiling the bottle parts for 5 minutes, and let them air dry.</li>
<li>Wash your hands first. Use a clean cup and spoon if you cannot sanitize bottles.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Warmth</h4>
<ul>
<li>Babies lose heat fast. Dress them in layers, put a hat on them, and use <strong>skin-to-skin contact</strong> against your chest under your clothes.</li>
<li>Do not overheat. In hot weather use light clothes and shade, and offer feeds often.</li>
<li>Do not use open flames, charcoal or camp stoves indoors to keep warm. See <a class="xl" data-id="Carbon Monoxide">Carbon Monoxide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Safe sleep</h4>
<ul>
<li>Lay the baby <strong>on their back, alone, on a firm, flat surface</strong> with no pillows, blankets or soft toys.</li>
<li>Do not sleep with the baby on a sofa or chair. If you must share a bed, keep soft bedding well away.</li>
<li>Do not wrap the baby in blankets that can cover the face.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Diapers and hygiene</h4>
<ul>
<li>Check diapers often. Change them promptly and use a plastic bag for waste. A baby with <strong>fewer than about 4 wet diapers a day</strong> may be dehydrated.</li>
<li>Wash hands after every change and before feeding. Use wipes or a cloth and safe water.</li>
<li>Keep spare diapers, wipes, formula, bottles, medicines and a change of clothes in your go-bag.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Seek help for</h4>
<p>A baby who feeds poorly, is very sleepy or floppy, has a fever, vomiting, diarrhea, blue color or trouble breathing. See <a class="xl" data-id="Children Are Different">Children Are Different</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Fever in Children">Fever in Children</a>.</p><p>Only change a tire on <strong>flat, firm ground</strong> well away from traffic. If you cannot, call for help.</p>
<h4>You need</h4>
<p>Spare tire (check its pressure), jack, lug wrench, and the car manual. A flashlight, gloves and a piece of wood to put under the jack on soft ground all help.</p>
<h4>Steps</h4>
<ol>
<li>Park on level ground, put it in Park, set the parking brake and turn on the hazard lights. Everyone gets out of the car and away from traffic.</li>
<li>Put a rock or block against the wheel <strong>opposite</strong> the flat so the car cannot roll.</li>
<li>Remove the hubcap. <strong>Loosen the lug nuts a half turn with the wrench while the wheel is still on the ground.</strong> Do not remove them yet.</li>
<li>Find the jack points in the manual. Place the jack on solid ground at the right point. Raise the car until the flat is about 5 cm (2 in) off the ground.</li>
<li>Remove the nuts and pull the wheel straight off.</li>
<li>Mount the spare on the studs. Put the nuts on by hand with the tapered sides facing in.</li>
<li>Tighten the nuts snugly in a <strong>star pattern</strong> (opposite nuts, not around in a circle).</li>
<li>Lower the car, remove the jack, then tighten all the nuts firmly as hard as you can, again in a star pattern.</li>
<li>Put the flat and tools away.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Important</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Never put any part of your body under a car that is held up only by a jack.</strong></li>
<li>Never start or run the engine with the car on the jack.</li>
<li>A small temporary spare ("donut") is only for short trips. Stay below about 80 km/h (50 mph) and get the proper tire fixed soon.</li>
<li>Check the spare's pressure and re-check the lug nuts after about 50 km (30 miles).</li>
<li>If a tire blows out while driving, hold the wheel firmly, ease off the gas and brake gently. Do not stomp on the brake.</li>
<li>A can of tire sealant or a plug kit can get you to a shop for a small puncture in the tread (not the sidewall).</li>
</ul>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Car Emergency Kit">Car Emergency Kit</a>.</p><p>Children are not small adults. They get sick faster, hide it well, and can get worse quickly. Use the other first aid guides, plus the points below.</p>
<h4>Fast checks</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Awake and responsive?</strong> Calling their name, touching them and watching for movement.</li>
<li><strong>Breathing:</strong> count breaths for 30 seconds and double it. Normal at rest is roughly 30 to 60 a minute for a baby, 24 to 40 for a toddler and 18 to 30 for a school-age child. Much faster, labored or noisy breathing, or skin pulling in between the ribs, is serious.</li>
<li><strong>Color:</strong> blue, gray or very pale lips and skin are an emergency.</li>
<li><strong>Drinking and wet diapers or urine:</strong> a child who will not drink or has not passed urine in 8 hours needs help.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Get help immediately for</h4>
<ul>
<li>Trouble breathing, bluish color, grunting with each breath</li>
<li>Unresponsive, very floppy, hard to wake, or seems confused</li>
<li>A seizure that lasts more than 5 minutes, or a first seizure. See <a class="xl" data-id="Seizures">Seizures</a>.</li>
<li>A baby under 3 months with a fever of 38 C (100.4 F) or higher. See <a class="xl" data-id="Fever in Children">Fever in Children</a>.</li>
<li>A head injury with vomiting, a severe headache, drowsiness or unequal pupils. See <a class="xl" data-id="Head Injuries and Concussion">Head Injuries and Concussion</a>.</li>
<li>A stiff neck, or a rash that does not fade when pressed with a glass</li>
<li>Swallowed medicine, batteries, magnets or chemicals. Call a poison center or emergency number at once.</li>
<li>Signs of allergic reaction. See <a class="xl" data-id="Severe Allergic Reaction (Anaphylaxis)">Severe Allergic Reaction (Anaphylaxis)</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Do not</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Never give aspirin to a child or teen.</strong> It can cause a dangerous illness.</li>
<li><strong>Never give honey to a baby under 1 year.</strong> It can cause botulism.</li>
<li>Do not give cough and cold medicine to young children.</li>
<li>Dose medicine by <strong>weight, not age</strong>, using the label and the measuring syringe or cup that came with it.</li>
<li><strong>Never shake a baby</strong>, even in frustration.</li>
</ul>
<h4>CPR, choking and fluids</h4>
<ul>
<li>CPR for a child and infant: see <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a>.</li>
<li>Choking in a child or infant: see <a class="xl" data-id="Choking (First Aid)">Choking</a>. Never do abdominal thrusts on a baby under 1.</li>
<li>Dehydration and rehydration drink: see <a class="xl" data-id="Diarrhea, Vomiting and Rehydration">Diarrhea, Vomiting and Rehydration</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Be kind and honest</h4>
<p>Get down to their level, speak calmly, tell them what you are doing and let them hold a toy or your hand. See <a class="xl" data-id="Helping Kids Cope">Helping Kids Cope</a>.</p><p>If the person can cough or speak, encourage them to keep coughing. Act when they <strong>cannot breathe, cough or speak</strong>.</p>
<h4>Adults and children over 1 year</h4>
<ol>
<li>Stand behind them. Lean them forward.</li>
<li>Give up to <strong>5 firm back blows</strong> between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand.</li>
<li>If that does not work, give up to <strong>5 abdominal thrusts</strong>: make a fist just above the belly button, grab it with your other hand, and pull sharply <strong>in and up</strong>.</li>
<li>Keep alternating 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out.</li>
<li>Use chest thrusts instead of abdominal thrusts for pregnant women and very large people.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Infants (under 1 year)</h4>
<ol>
<li>Lay the baby face down along your forearm, head lower than the body, supporting the jaw.</li>
<li>Give 5 back blows between the shoulder blades.</li>
<li>Turn the baby face up and give 5 chest thrusts with two fingers in the center of the chest.</li>
<li>Repeat. Never do abdominal thrusts on an infant.</li>
</ol>
<h4>If they become unresponsive</h4>
<p>Lower them to the ground and start <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a>. Look in the mouth before rescue breaths and remove any object you can see. Do not sweep blindly with your finger.</p>
<blockquote>Anyone who received abdominal thrusts should be checked by a medical professional when possible.</blockquote><blockquote>A choking dog may bite out of panic. Be careful with your hands.</blockquote>
<p>Signs: pawing at the mouth, gagging, blue or pale gums, wheezing, panic.</p>
<h4>Do this now</h4>
<ul>
<li>Open the mouth and look. If you can see the object and it is easy to reach, hook it out with a finger. Do not push it deeper.</li>
<li>Small dog: hold it with its head down and give firm pats between the shoulder blades.</li>
<li>Larger dog: lift the back legs so the head tilts down, then give firm pats between the shoulder blades.</li>
<li>If that fails: put both hands behind the ribs and give quick, firm thrusts up and forward.</li>
<li>Go to a vet right away, even if the object comes out.</li>
</ul><p>When phones fail, radio often still works. Each type has a different job.</p>
<h4>Radios that only listen</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>AM/FM radio:</strong> local news and official instructions. A battery or hand-crank model is the first thing to own.</li>
<li><strong>NOAA weather radio:</strong> automatic weather and hazard alerts. See <a class="xl" data-id="NOAA Weather Radio and Alerts">NOAA Weather Radio and Alerts</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Shortwave:</strong> news from far away, useful if local stations are down.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Radios that talk</h4>
<p>| Type | Range (typical) | License (US) | Good for |<br>|---|---|---|---|<br>| <strong>FRS</strong> walkie-talkie | 1 to 3 km (0.5 to 2 mi) | none | family, neighbors |<br>| <strong>GMRS</strong> | 3 to 8 km (2 to 5 mi), more with repeaters | license, no exam | families, neighborhoods, vehicles |<br>| <strong>MURS</strong> | 2 to 5 km (1 to 3 mi) | none | small groups, farms |<br>| <strong>CB</strong> | 5 to 15 km (3 to 10 mi) | none | highways, trucks |<br>| <strong>Ham (amateur)</strong> handheld | 5 to 50 km (3 to 30+ mi), with repeaters | license with exam | wide-area help, nets |<br>| <strong>Mesh (this device)</strong> | a few hundred meters per hop | none | text between boards |</p>
<p>Range is shorter in hills, forests and buildings. See <a class="xl" data-id="Getting More Range from a Radio">Getting More Range</a>.</p>
<h4>What to keep</h4>
<ul>
<li>Two or more matching <strong>FRS/GMRS handhelds</strong> for family and neighbors. See <a class="xl" data-id="FRS and GMRS Walkie-Talkies">FRS and GMRS Walkie-Talkies</a>.</li>
<li>A <strong>battery or hand-crank AM/FM/NOAA radio.</strong></li>
<li>Spare batteries, or a power bank and cable. Rechargeable handhelds need a way to charge.</li>
<li>A <strong>ham handheld</strong> if you or a neighbor is licensed. See <a class="xl" data-id="Ham Radio in an Emergency">Ham Radio in an Emergency</a>.</li>
<li>Pen and paper. Write down frequencies, call signs and messages.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Licenses</h4>
<p>Rules differ by country. In the US, FRS, MURS and CB need no license. GMRS needs a simple license with no exam, and amateur radio needs an exam. Learn and practice <strong>before</strong> a disaster. In a real, life-threatening emergency with no other way to get help, calling for help on any radio you have is widely accepted.</p>
<h4>Good habits</h4>
<ul>
<li>Test radios monthly. Charge them. Keep them in a bag with spare batteries.</li>
<li>Agree on a <strong>channel and a check-in time</strong> with your family before you need them.</li>
<li>Keep transmissions short to save batteries and keep the channel free for others.</li>
</ul><p>Short-coated and small dogs lose heat fast. Ears, tail, and paws are first to suffer.</p>
<h4>Signs of trouble</h4>
<ul>
<li>Shivering, slowing down, whining, lifting paws, refusing to walk.</li>
<li>Pale or gray skin on ears, tail, or paws can be frostbite.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Do this</h4>
<ul>
<li>Get indoors. Wrap in dry blankets. Warm gently with room-temperature (not hot) water and body heat.</li>
<li>Do not rub frostbitten skin. Do not use a heating pad directly.</li>
<li>See a vet for frostbite or if the dog is very sleepy.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prevention</h4>
<ul>
<li>Coat or sweater for short-haired dogs. Booties for ice and salt.</li>
<li>Wipe paws after walks. Road salt and de-icer can poison.</li>
<li>Never leave a dog in a cold car.</li>
</ul><h4>Stay warm with <strong>COLD</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>C - Clean:</strong> dirty clothes lose insulation.</li>
<li><strong>O - avoid Overheating:</strong> remove layers before you sweat.</li>
<li><strong>L - Loose layers:</strong> tight clothing cuts circulation.</li>
<li><strong>D - Dry:</strong> wet clothing pulls heat away fast. Dry socks matter most.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Snow trench</h4>
<ol>
<li>Dig a trench as long as your body, about 1 m (3 ft) deep, in firm snow.</li>
<li>Roof it with branches, skis or a tarp, then pile snow on top.</li>
<li>Line the floor with thick insulation: boughs, packs, foam.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Quinzee (snow mound)</h4>
<ol>
<li>Pile snow into a mound about 2 m (7 ft) high. Mix layers as you pile.</li>
<li>Let it settle for 1-2 hours.</li>
<li>Push sticks about 30 cm (1 ft) into the mound all over, to show wall thickness.</li>
<li>Dig a low entrance and hollow it out until you hit the sticks.</li>
<li>Poke a fist-sized <strong>air hole</strong> in the roof.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Always make an air vent in any snow shelter and keep it clear. Keep a digging tool inside with you.</blockquote>
<h4>Tree well</h4>
<p>Under a large evergreen, snow is often shallow near the trunk. Dig out the space and roof it with boughs.</p>
<h4>Body heat tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Eat before sleeping; digestion makes heat.</li>
<li>Do light exercise (not to the point of sweating) before getting in your shelter.</li>
<li>Put a bottle of hot water inside your clothes or bag.</li>
<li>Share body heat with others.</li>
</ul><h4>Cook on coals, not flames</h4>
<p>Flames burn the outside and leave the inside raw. Let a fire burn down to a bed of glowing coals, or rake coals to one side of the fire to cook on while the other side keeps burning.</p>
<p><strong>Hand test for heat:</strong> hold your palm about 10 cm (4 in) above the coals.</p>
<ul>
<li>2 seconds before it is too hot: very hot</li>
<li>4-5 seconds: medium, good for most cooking</li>
<li>6-8 seconds: low, good for simmering</li>
</ul>
<h4>Methods</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Boiling:</strong> the safest way to cook anything you are unsure about, and it makes water safe. Hang the pot from a tripod or support it on rocks or green logs.</li>
<li><strong>Foil packets:</strong> wrap food in two layers of foil and set it on coals. Turn once. Vegetables take 15-20 minutes, fish about 10.</li>
<li><strong>Skewers and spits:</strong> use green (live) wood, so it does not burn through. Avoid poisonous woods like yew and oleander.</li>
<li><strong>Hot stone:</strong> heat a flat, dry stone at the edge of the fire and cook on it like a pan.</li>
<li><strong>Pit cooking:</strong> heat stones in a fire in a pit, rake out the fire, wrap food in green leaves, cover with soil and leave for hours.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Never heat rocks from rivers, lakes or wet ground. Trapped water can make them crack or explode.</blockquote>
<h4>Cook meat fully</h4>
<p>Wild meat and fish can carry parasites. Cook until juices run clear and there is no pink.</p>
<ul>
<li>Poultry and birds: 74 C (165 F) inside</li>
<li>Ground or chopped meat: 71 C (160 F)</li>
<li>Whole cuts of game: 71 C (160 F) is safest for wild meat</li>
<li>Fish: until it flakes easily and looks opaque</li>
</ul>
<h4>Safety</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep a pot of water or a pile of soil next to the cooking fire.</li>
<li>Use a stick or cloth to handle hot pots; metal handles get very hot.</li>
<li>Wash hands before handling food, and keep raw meat away from cooked food.</li>
</ul><p>Start CPR if a person is <strong>unresponsive and not breathing normally</strong> (or only gasping).</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/cpr.jpg" alt="Hands-only CPR" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Adults</h4>
<ol>
<li>Call for help. If someone else is there, send them to call and to find an AED (defibrillator).</li>
<li>Lay the person on their back on a firm surface.</li>
<li>Put the heel of one hand on the <strong>center of the chest</strong> (lower half of the breastbone), the other hand on top.</li>
<li>Arms straight, shoulders over your hands. Push <strong>hard and fast</strong>:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li><strong>Depth:</strong> 5-6 cm (2-2.4 in)</li>
<li><strong>Rate:</strong> 100-120 per minute (the beat of the song "Stayin' Alive")</li>
<li>Let the chest come all the way back up between pushes.</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>If trained and willing: after every 30 compressions, give 2 rescue breaths (head tilt, chin lift, pinch nose, breathe until the chest rises).</li>
<li>If not trained: keep doing compressions only. That still saves lives.</li>
<li>Keep going until the person starts breathing, an AED is ready, help takes over, or you are too exhausted to continue.</li>
</ol>
<h4>AED</h4>
<p>Turn it on and follow the voice instructions. Keep compressions going while it is being set up.</p>
<h4>Children and infants</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Child:</strong> one or two hands, about 5 cm (2 in) deep.</li>
<li><strong>Infant:</strong> two fingers in the center of the chest just below the nipple line, about 4 cm (1.5 in) deep.</li>
<li>For children and drowning victims, rescue breaths are especially important if you can give them.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>In remote places with no chance of advanced care, CPR rarely restarts the heart after a long time. It is still right to try. Your safety and the group's safety come first.</blockquote><blockquote>Only for an unresponsive dog that is not breathing. Call a vet on the way. Someone else should drive if possible.</blockquote>
<h4>Steps</h4>
<ul>
<li>Lay the dog on its right side on a firm surface.</li>
<li>Chest compressions: hands over the widest part of the chest, push about one third to one half of the chest depth, 100 to 120 per minute.</li>
<li>Small dogs and puppies: use one hand or two fingers around the chest.</li>
<li>Rescue breaths: close the mouth, breathe into the nose until the chest rises. Two breaths after every 30 compressions.</li>
<li>Keep going until the dog responds or you reach a vet.</li>
</ul>
<p>Learn this in a certified pet first aid class. This card is a reminder, not training.</p><blockquote>Never leave a tight band on. Check often that the paw or limb is not cold or swollen.</blockquote>
<h4>Bleeding</h4>
<ul>
<li>Press a clean cloth or gauze firmly on the wound. Keep pressing. Do not peek.</li>
<li>Add more cloth over the top if it soaks through.</li>
<li>Wrap snugly with self-sticking wrap (not tight). Leave the toes showing so you can check them.</li>
<li>Get to a vet for deep cuts, anything that will not stop in a few minutes, or any puncture.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Paws</h4>
<ul>
<li>Rinse dirt out with clean water.</li>
<li>Cover with a clean sock or bootie to keep it clean on the way to the vet.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Stop the licking</h4>
<ul>
<li>Licking reopens wounds. Use a cone or a clean sock until a vet looks at it.</li>
</ul><p>Most wild animals avoid people. Encounters usually happen when an animal is surprised, defending young or food, or used to human food.</p>
<h4>Avoid them</h4>
<ul>
<li>Make noise in thick brush and near streams so animals hear you coming.</li>
<li>Never leave food, trash or toiletries in your shelter. See <a class="xl" data-id="Keeping Food and Camp Safe">Keeping Food and Camp Safe</a>.</li>
<li>Travel in groups during the day. Keep children close.</li>
<li>Give all animals space, especially mothers with young.</li>
<li>Carry <strong>bear spray</strong> in bear country, where you can reach it in a second.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Bears - general rules</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not run.</strong> Bears are much faster than people, and running can trigger a chase.</li>
<li>Stay calm and talk in a low, firm voice so it knows you are human.</li>
<li>Pick up small children. Stand together as a group.</li>
<li>Back away slowly, sideways, while watching the bear.</li>
<li>If it approaches, use bear spray when it is about 10 m (30 ft) away: aim slightly down so the cloud is between you and the bear.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If a bear attacks</h4>
<p><strong>Defensive attack</strong> (surprised bear, often a grizzly or brown bear, or a mother with cubs):</p>
<ul>
<li>Play dead. Lie flat on your stomach, hands clasped behind your neck, legs apart so it is hard to flip you. Keep your pack on.</li>
<li>Stay still until the bear has left the area.</li>
<li>If it keeps attacking for a long time, fight back hard.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Predatory attack</strong> (a bear stalks you, follows you, or attacks at night in your tent - often a black bear):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fight back</strong> with everything you have. Hit the face and nose with rocks, sticks, anything.</li>
<li>Do not play dead.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cougars (mountain lions)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not run, and do not turn your back.</li>
<li>Make yourself look big: raise your arms, open your jacket, stand on a rock.</li>
<li>Shout, throw things, and back away slowly.</li>
<li>If attacked, <strong>fight back</strong> and protect your neck. Stay on your feet if you can.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Moose and other large plant-eaters</h4>
<p>Moose injure more people than bears in some regions. If ears go back and hair on its hump stands up, it is angry. Run and get behind a tree or vehicle: moose usually stop chasing quickly.</p>
<h4>Dogs</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not run or stare. Stand still, side-on, hands close to your body.</li>
<li>Say "No" or "Go home" firmly.</li>
<li>If attacked, put something between you and the dog (a pack, jacket or stick). If knocked down, curl into a ball and protect your face and neck.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Any animal bite or scratch that breaks the skin needs thorough washing for 15 minutes with soap and water, and medical care as soon as possible because of rabies and infection.</blockquote><p>The debris hut keeps you warm with no fire, sleeping bag or tools. It works like a giant sleeping bag made of leaves.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/debris_hut.jpg" alt="Debris hut cross-section" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Building it</h4>
<ol>
<li>Find a <strong>ridge pole</strong> a bit longer than your height.</li>
<li>Prop one end on a stump, rock or a pair of crossed sticks about waist high. The other end rests on the ground.</li>
<li>Lie down under it to check: it should be just big enough to fit you. Bigger means colder.</li>
<li>Lean sticks along both sides of the pole like ribs, close together.</li>
<li>Lay a lattice of small branches over the ribs so leaves do not fall through.</li>
<li>Pile on dry leaves, grass, pine needles and debris until the walls are <strong>60-90 cm (2-3 ft) thick</strong>. Thicker for colder nights.</li>
<li>Add some branches on top to keep the wind from blowing the leaves off.</li>
<li>Stuff the inside with dry leaves for a thick bed, and keep a pile by the entrance.</li>
<li>Crawl in feet first and pull the leaf pile in to plug the door.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>It takes a lot of debris, often a few hours of work. Start early and gather more than you think you need.</blockquote><h4>How much you need</h4>
<ul>
<li>Resting in mild weather: about <strong>2-3 liters a day</strong>.</li>
<li>Heat or hard work: <strong>4-6 liters or more</strong>.</li>
<li>Cold and high altitude also dry you out faster than you expect.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Do not ration water you already have. Drink what you need and work to find more. People have died of dehydration with water still in their bottles.</blockquote>
<h4>Signs of dehydration</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Early:</strong> thirst, dark yellow urine, dry mouth, headache, tiredness.</li>
<li><strong>Worse:</strong> little or no urine, dizziness, fast heartbeat, confusion.</li>
<li><strong>Severe:</strong> fainting, no sweat in heat, sunken eyes. This is an emergency.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check your urine. Pale yellow means you are getting enough.</p>
<h4>Save water inside your body</h4>
<ul>
<li>Rest in the shade during the hottest hours. Travel at night or early morning.</li>
<li>Keep clothes on in the sun; they slow sweating.</li>
<li>Breathe through your nose and talk less.</li>
<li>Avoid alcohol, caffeine and salty or very high-protein food when water is short.</li>
<li>If water is very limited, eat little. Digestion uses water.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Rehydrating</h4>
<ul>
<li>Sip steadily rather than gulping large amounts.</li>
<li>If someone has been sweating heavily, add a pinch of salt and a little sugar to a liter of water. Commercial oral rehydration salts are best.</li>
</ul><p>In heat, shelter means <strong>shade</strong>. The goal is to save water by not sweating.</p>
<h4>Simple shade</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use natural shade: rock overhangs, the shady side of boulders, bushes.</li>
<li>Hang a tarp or blanket 30-45 cm (12-18 in) above the ground, with a second layer above it if you have it. The air gap between layers cuts heat a lot.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Below-ground shelter</h4>
<ol>
<li>Dig a trench 45-60 cm (18-24 in) deep, as long as your body, oriented east-west.</li>
<li>Pile the sand around the edges.</li>
<li>Cover with a tarp, then a second layer with a gap between them.</li>
</ol>
<p>The ground 30 cm down can be much cooler than the surface. Dig at dawn or dusk, not midday.</p>
<h4>Habits</h4>
<ul>
<li>Rest in the heat of the day; work and travel at night or early morning.</li>
<li>Keep your skin covered with loose, light-colored clothing and a hat.</li>
<li>Do not lie directly on hot ground. Sit on a pack or brush.</li>
<li>Watch for snakes and scorpions in shade spots; check before you reach in.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Cars, metal sheds and tents in full sun can become deadly hot. Shade with airflow is better.</blockquote><h4>Low blood sugar (the common emergency)</h4>
<p><strong>Signs:</strong> shaky, sweating, pale, hungry, fast heartbeat, confused, cranky or acting drunk, then seizures or passing out.</p>
<p><strong>If they are awake and can swallow:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Give <strong>15-20 g of fast sugar</strong>: 3-4 glucose tablets, half a cup (120 ml) of juice or regular soda, or a tablespoon of sugar or honey.</li>
<li>Wait 15 minutes and recheck. If still unwell, give the same again.</li>
<li>Once better, give a snack with starch and protein, such as crackers and peanut butter.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>If they are unconscious or cannot swallow:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not put food or drink in the mouth.</strong> Place them on their side and <strong>call emergency services</strong>.</li>
<li>If they carry glucagon and you know how, give it.</li>
</ul>
<h4>High blood sugar</h4>
<ul>
<li>Signs: very thirsty, passing lots of urine, tired, nausea, fruity breath, deep fast breathing, drowsy.</li>
<li>Give water if they are awake. Needs medical care, especially with vomiting or confusion.</li>
</ul>
<h4>When you are not sure</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Treat it as low blood sugar.</strong> Sugar helps a low and does little harm in a high, while untreated lows are dangerous.</li>
</ul>
<h4>In a long outage</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Insulin</strong> can be kept at room temperature (below about 25 C / 77 F) for about 28 days. Keep it out of sun and heat. Do not let it freeze.</li>
<li>Keep sugar tablets handy and know the signs of a low. Eat regular meals and do not skip doses.</li>
<li>Do not take their insulin or medicine unless you know what to do.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>A diabetic person who seems drunk, confused or odd should get sugar first and questions later.</blockquote><p>In survival settings, diarrhea from bad water or food is common, and dehydration from it can be deadly, especially for children and older people.</p>
<h4>Rehydration drink</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/ors_recipe.jpg" alt="Homemade oral rehydration solution" loading="lazy"></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>1 liter</strong> of clean, safe water</li>
<li><strong>6 level teaspoons</strong> of sugar</li>
<li><strong>1/2 level teaspoon</strong> of salt</li>
</ul>
<p>Stir until dissolved. Measure carefully: too much salt is harmful. Commercial oral rehydration salts (ORS) are best if you have them.</p>
<h4>How to give it</h4>
<ul>
<li>Small sips often, every few minutes, even if they are vomiting. Most of it will stay down.</li>
<li>Adults: aim for at least a cup after each loose stool, on top of normal drinking.</li>
<li>Children: a quarter to half cup after each loose stool, by spoon if needed.</li>
<li>Keep breastfeeding babies.</li>
<li>Once vomiting settles, start eating simple foods: rice, porridge, bread, bananas, soup.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Danger signs - get help</h4>
<ul>
<li>Blood in the stool or black, tarry stool</li>
<li>High fever</li>
<li>No urine for 8 hours or more, very dark urine</li>
<li>Very sleepy, confused, or unable to drink</li>
<li>Sunken eyes, dry mouth, no tears (in children)</li>
<li>Diarrhea lasting more than 3 days, or vomiting that will not stop for a day</li>
</ul>
<h4>Stop it spreading</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wash hands</strong> with soap after the toilet and before handling food or water.</li>
<li>Dig toilets at least 60 m (200 ft) from water sources, camp and trails, and 15-20 cm (6-8 in) deep. Cover waste completely.</li>
<li>Treat all drinking water. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making Water Safe to Drink">Making Water Safe to Drink</a>.</li>
<li>Keep a separate cup and towel for the sick person.</li>
</ul><h4>Pack for your dog</h4>
<ul>
<li>Water and a collapsible bowl.</li>
<li>At least a week of food in a sealed container.</li>
<li>Medications and a note of the dose and schedule.</li>
<li>Leash, spare leash, and a harness that fits.</li>
<li>Vaccination records, microchip number, and a recent photo of your dog.</li>
<li>Waste bags, towel, and an old blanket or bed.</li>
<li>Booties, a muzzle (a hurt dog can bite), and a first-aid kit.</li>
<li>Collar with ID tags that have your cell phone number.</li>
<li>A crate or carrier that fits your dog.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check expiry dates twice a year. Keep the bag by the door with the human go-bag.</p><p>A fallen line can still be live. It does not have to spark or buzz to be deadly. The ground around it can also carry current.</p>
<h4>If you see a downed line</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stay at least 10 m (33 ft) away</strong>, about the length of a bus, and keep other people back too.</li>
<li>Do not touch the line, or anything touching it: trees, fences, puddles, vehicles, branches.</li>
<li><strong>Call the power company and 911.</strong> Keep watching until help arrives so no one walks into it.</li>
<li>Do not try to move a line with a stick or tool.</li>
<li>Treat every line as live, including cable, phone and "dead-looking" lines. They may be touching a live power line.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If a line falls on your car</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stay inside.</strong> Honk the horn or call for help and wait for the utility to turn the power off.</li>
<li>Warn others to stay away.</li>
<li><strong>Only get out if the car is on fire.</strong> In that case:</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>Open the door and <strong>jump clear</strong> with both feet together, without touching the car and ground at the same time.</li>
<li><strong>Shuffle away</strong> with your feet together and touching the ground, in small steps. Do not take long strides. The ground near the line has different voltages, and a long step can shock you.</li>
<li>Keep going until you are at least 10 m (33 ft) away.</li>
</ol>
<h4>If you are near a line and feel a tingle</h4>
<p>Your feet are in a danger area. <strong>Shuffle away</strong> with feet together, in small steps.</p>
<h4>After storms and floods</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not drive through or walk into water near a downed line.</li>
<li>Report downed lines even if no one is hurt.</li>
<li>Do not restore power at home if there is damage or flooding. See <a class="xl" data-id="Utility Shutoffs">Utility Shutoffs</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>First aid</h4>
<p>If someone is shocked, <strong>do not touch them</strong> until the power is off. Then call for help, check breathing and begin CPR if needed. See <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Burns">Burns</a>.</p><h4>Rescue safely</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Shout for help and call emergency services</strong> if you can.</li>
<li><strong>Reach or throw. Do not go in.</strong> Drowning people grab and pull rescuers under.</li>
<li>Reach with a pole, branch, paddle or clothing. Throw a rope, a ring, a cooler, a jug or anything that floats.</li>
<li>Only swim out as a last resort, and only if you are a strong swimmer. Bring something that floats and keep it between you and them.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Once they are out</h4>
<ol>
<li>Lay them on their back on firm ground. Check for breathing for no more than 10 seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Not breathing:</strong> start with <strong>5 rescue breaths</strong>, then CPR (30 compressions, 2 breaths). Drowning is a lack of air, so breaths matter more than usual.</li>
<li>Keep going until they breathe, help takes over, or you cannot continue.</li>
<li>If they vomit, roll them onto their side, clear the mouth, then roll back and continue.</li>
</ol>
<h4>If they are breathing</h4>
<ul>
<li>Roll them onto their <strong>side</strong> (recovery position) and keep them warm. Wet clothes off, dry layers on.</li>
<li>Stay with them and watch their breathing.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Always get medical help</h4>
<ul>
<li>Anyone who needed rescue, coughed a lot, or was under water, even if they seem fine now. Lungs can fill with fluid hours later.</li>
<li>Watch for a cough that will not stop, fast breathing, blue lips, unusual sleepiness or confusion.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cold water</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cold water drowning victims have been revived after a long time under. <strong>Do not give up early.</strong> Keep going with CPR and rewarm them.</li>
<li>If someone is very cold, handle them gently and keep them flat.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Suspect a neck or back injury after a dive or fall. Keep the head, neck and body in a line as you move them, unless they are not breathing.</blockquote><h4>During the shaking: Drop, Cover, Hold On</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Drop</strong> to your hands and knees right where you are.</li>
<li><strong>Cover</strong> your head and neck with your arms. Get under a sturdy table or desk if one is within a few steps. If not, crawl next to an interior wall away from windows.</li>
<li><strong>Hold on</strong> to your shelter and move with it until the shaking stops.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stay where you are.</strong> Most injuries happen when people run or try to leave a building.</li>
<li><strong>In bed:</strong> stay there and cover your head with a pillow.</li>
<li><strong>Do not stand in a doorway.</strong> It is no stronger than other parts of a house.</li>
<li><strong>Outside:</strong> move to open space away from buildings, trees, streetlights and power lines, then drop, cover and hold on.</li>
<li><strong>In a car:</strong> pull over in a clear area, away from bridges, overpasses and wires. Stay inside with your seat belt on until the shaking stops.</li>
<li><strong>Do not use elevators.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h4>Coastal areas</h4>
<p>If the shaking is strong or lasts a long time, or you are told to, <strong>go to high ground or inland at once</strong>. A tsunami can arrive within minutes. Do not wait for an official warning.</p>
<h4>After the shaking</h4>
<ul>
<li>Expect aftershocks. Drop, cover and hold on again each time.</li>
<li>Check yourself and others for injuries. See <a class="xl" data-id="First Aid Priorities">First Aid Priorities</a>.</li>
<li>Put on sturdy shoes and watch for broken glass.</li>
<li><strong>If you smell gas or hear hissing, get out and call from outside.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Gas Leak">Gas Leak</a>. Do not light matches or flip switches.</li>
<li>Check for fires and put out small ones. See <a class="xl" data-id="Fire Safety">Fire Safety</a>.</li>
<li>Check the building for cracks and damage. Leave if it looks unsafe.</li>
<li>Use text messages instead of calls.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are trapped</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not light a match. Do not move around, which kicks up dust.</li>
<li>Cover your mouth with cloth.</li>
<li>Tap on a pipe or wall, or use a whistle, so rescuers can find you. Shout only as a last resort, because it makes you inhale dust.</li>
</ul><h4>Make it safe first</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Do not touch the person</strong> while they may still be in contact with power.</li>
<li>Turn the power off at the switch, breaker or meter if you can reach it safely. Unplug the cord if the plug is dry and undamaged.</li>
<li>If you cannot cut the power, push the source away with something <strong>dry and not metal</strong>, such as a wooden broom handle or a dry rope. Stand on something dry.</li>
<li><strong>Downed power lines:</strong> stay at least 10 m (33 ft) away. Ground near a line can carry current. Shuffle away with feet together if you feel a tingle. Call the power company and emergency services.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Then</h4>
<ol>
<li>Check if they respond and are breathing. Call emergency services.</li>
<li><strong>Not breathing:</strong> start CPR.</li>
<li>Cool burns with running water for 10-20 minutes. Cover them loosely with clean, non-stick cloth. Look for a second burn where the current left the body.</li>
<li>Lay them down, keep them warm and treat for shock. Do not move them if you suspect a neck or back injury.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Get medical help for everyone who was shocked</h4>
<ul>
<li>The heart can be affected even if they feel fine. Anyone with high voltage, a long contact, burns, faintness, chest pain or an irregular pulse needs a hospital.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Lightning</h4>
<ul>
<li>A person struck by lightning carries no charge. It is safe to touch them.</li>
<li>Many strikes stop the heart or breathing, and CPR works. If several people are hurt, treat those who are not breathing first.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Never touch a car that has a power line on it. Stay inside unless it is on fire, then jump clear with both feet together and shuffle away.</blockquote><h4>How much</h4>
<p>At least <strong>3 days</strong> of food for everyone in the home. <strong>Two weeks</strong> is better. Include pets.</p>
<h4>What to store</h4>
<p>Choose food your family already eats, that needs little or no cooking and little water:</p>
<ul>
<li>Canned meat, fish, beans, soups, vegetables and fruit</li>
<li>Peanut butter, nuts, trail mix</li>
<li>Crackers, granola and energy bars</li>
<li>Dried fruit</li>
<li>Ready-to-eat cereal, instant oats</li>
<li>Shelf-stable milk or powdered milk</li>
<li>Rice, pasta and dried beans (only if you can cook with water to spare)</li>
<li>Comfort food: chocolate, coffee, tea, sweets. Morale matters.</li>
<li>Baby food and formula if you have an infant; special foods for anyone with allergies or medical needs</li>
</ul>
<h4>Do not forget</h4>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>manual can opener</strong></li>
<li>Plates, cups and utensils you can use without washing</li>
<li>A way to cook without power: camp stove and fuel, used <strong>outdoors only</strong></li>
</ul>
<h4>Keeping it fresh</h4>
<ul>
<li>Store in a cool, dry, dark place, off the floor.</li>
<li><strong>First in, first out:</strong> use the oldest food first and put new purchases at the back.</li>
<li>Check dates twice a year (for example, when the clocks change).</li>
<li>Throw out cans that are bulging, leaking, rusted deeply or dented along a seam.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Salty foods make you thirsty. If water is short, choose low-salt foods and canned foods packed in water or juice.</blockquote><h4>At home when the water is off</h4>
<p><strong>Bucket toilet:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Use a 5-gallon (20 L) bucket with a snug lid, or line a toilet bowl with a heavy trash bag.</li>
<li>Add a little <strong>kitty litter, sawdust, ash or dry soil</strong> after each use to cut smell and flies.</li>
<li>Tie the bag shut when it is half full. Double-bag it and store it in a lidded container outdoors away from the house, well and animals.</li>
<li>Wash your hands afterward. See the handwashing guide.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Do not</strong> flush if sewers may be backed up or the water is off. Do not pour waste near wells, streams or garden food.</p>
<h4>In the field</h4>
<p><strong>Cathole (short stays, small groups):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Dig a hole about 15-20 cm (6-8 in) deep, <strong>at least 60 m (200 ft)</strong> from water, trails and camp.</li>
<li>Cover it fully with soil when done.</li>
<li>Carry used paper out in a bag, or burn it completely. Never leave it on top.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Trench latrine (a group staying several days):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Dig a trench about 30 cm (1 ft) wide and 60-90 cm (2-3 ft) deep, downhill and downwind of camp and water. Cover each use with soil. Fill it in when you leave.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Keep people healthy</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wash hands after every trip and before handling food.</li>
<li>Keep flies off waste and food. Cover the toilet.</li>
<li>Dispose of diapers, bandages and wipes in sealed bags, not in the trench.</li>
<li>Sick people with diarrhea need their own bucket. Clean it with a bleach solution (1 part bleach to 9 parts water).</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Poor sanitation causes more sickness than most dangers on this list. Treat it as a first-day priority.</blockquote><p>Learn a few knots well rather than many badly. Practice them until you can tie them with cold hands or in the dark.</p>
<h4>Bowline - a loop that will not slip</h4>
<p>Use it for a fixed loop: tying a line around a tree, a rescue loop, hanging a food bag.</p>
<ol>
<li>Make a small loop in the standing part of the rope, with the working end lying on top.</li>
<li>Pass the working end <strong>up through</strong> the small loop from underneath.</li>
<li>Take it <strong>around behind</strong> the standing part.</li>
<li>Bring it back <strong>down through</strong> the small loop.</li>
<li>Hold the working end against the big loop and pull the standing part to tighten.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Leave a tail at least 10 cm (4 in) long. A bowline can loosen when it is not under load, so check it.</blockquote>
<h4>Clove hitch - quick tie to a pole</h4>
<p>Use it to start lashings or tie a line to a post.</p>
<ol>
<li>Wrap the rope once around the pole.</li>
<li>Cross over the first wrap and go around again.</li>
<li>Tuck the end under the second wrap, so both ends come out between the wraps in opposite directions.</li>
<li>Pull both ends tight.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Taut-line hitch - an adjustable tent line</h4>
<p>Lets you tighten or loosen a guy line without retying.</p>
<ol>
<li>Pass the rope around a stake or anchor.</li>
<li>Wrap the working end <strong>twice</strong> around the standing part, working back toward the anchor, inside the loop.</li>
<li>Make <strong>one more wrap</strong> around the standing part on the outside of those two wraps.</li>
<li>Tighten. Slide the knot to adjust the tension. It holds under load.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Square (reef) knot - tie two ends together</h4>
<p>For bandages, bundles and packages.</p>
<ol>
<li>Right end over left, and tuck under.</li>
<li>Left end over right, and tuck under.</li>
<li>Both ends should lie flat, alongside their own parts.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Do not use a square knot to join two ropes that will carry weight. It can slip or spill. Use a sheet bend.</blockquote>
<h4>Sheet bend - join two ropes</h4>
<p>Works even with ropes of different thickness.</p>
<ol>
<li>Make a bend (U shape) in the end of the thicker rope.</li>
<li>Pass the thinner rope <strong>up through</strong> the bend from behind.</li>
<li>Wrap it <strong>around behind</strong> both sides of the bend.</li>
<li>Tuck it <strong>under itself</strong>, not back through the bend. Pull tight.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Trucker's hitch - pull a line very tight</h4>
<p>Great for ridgelines and tying down loads.</p>
<ol>
<li>Tie one end to an anchor.</li>
<li>Partway along the line, twist a small loop into the rope (a slip loop works).</li>
<li>Run the free end around the second anchor and back up through that loop.</li>
<li>Pull down on the free end. The loop acts like a pulley and gives you about twice the pull.</li>
<li>Hold the tension and lock it off with two half hitches below the loop.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Rope care</h4>
<ul>
<li>Melt or tape the ends of synthetic cord so they do not fray.</li>
<li>Keep rope out of fire and away from sharp edges.</li>
<li>Paracord can be pulled apart: the inner strands make fishing line, sewing thread or snare line.</li>
</ul><p>Heat kills more people than most other weather. Older adults, small children, outdoor workers and people with health problems are at the highest risk.</p>
<h4>Stay cool</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stay in air conditioning if you can. A few hours a day in a cool place (a library, mall or cooling center) helps your body.</li>
<li>Without AC, stay on the lowest floor out of the sun. Close curtains and blinds on sunny windows.</li>
<li>Take cool showers or baths. Wet your skin, hair and clothes and sit in front of a fan.</li>
<li>A fan alone does not cool you when air is above about 35 C (95 F). Use water on your skin too.</li>
<li>Wear light, loose, light-colored clothing and a wide hat.</li>
<li><strong>Do hard work in the cool early morning or evening.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h4>Drink</h4>
<ul>
<li>Drink water before you feel thirsty. See <a class="xl" data-id="Dehydration and Water Needs">Dehydration and Water Needs</a>.</li>
<li>Avoid alcohol and large amounts of sugary drinks.</li>
<li>If you sweat a lot, replace salt too, with food or an oral rehydration drink.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Never leave anyone in a parked car</h4>
<p>Inside temperatures can reach deadly levels in minutes, even with the windows cracked. This applies to <strong>children and pets</strong>. Look in the back seat every time you leave the car.</p>
<h4>Check on others</h4>
<p>Call or visit older neighbors and people who live alone at least twice a day.</p>
<h4>Know the warning signs</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heat exhaustion:</strong> heavy sweating, cold, clammy skin, weakness, headache, nausea.</li>
<li><strong>Heat stroke is an emergency:</strong> hot, red, dry or damp skin, confusion, fainting, a body temperature above 40 C (104 F). <strong>Call for help and cool the person right away.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Heat Exhaustion and Heat Stroke">Heat Exhaustion and Heat Stroke</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If the power fails</h4>
<p>Move to the coolest part of the house or a cooling center. Use wet cloths on the neck, armpits and groin. See <a class="xl" data-id="Desert and Hot Weather Shelter">Desert and Hot Weather Shelter</a>.</p><h4>Dust or grit in the eye</h4>
<ol>
<li>Do not rub it.</li>
<li>Blink several times and let tears wash it out.</li>
<li>Flush with clean water from the inner corner (nose side) outward.</li>
<li>Pull the upper lid down over the lower lashes, which can sweep the speck off.</li>
<li>If it is on the white of the eye, lift it gently with the corner of a clean, wet cloth.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Chemicals in the eye</h4>
<p>Flush <strong>immediately</strong> with lots of clean water for at least <strong>15-20 minutes</strong>, holding the eye open, with the injured eye lower so water does not run into the other eye. Then get medical help.</p>
<h4>Object stuck in the eye</h4>
<blockquote>Do not try to remove anything stuck in the eye.</blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Keep the person still and calm.</li>
<li>Cover the injured eye with a paper cup or similar shield taped in place, without pressing on the eye.</li>
<li>Cover the other eye too. Eyes move together, so this keeps the injured eye still.</li>
<li>Get medical help urgently.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Blow to the eye</h4>
<p>Cool with a cold cloth for 15 minutes, without pressure. Seek help for vision changes, blood inside the colored part of the eye, severe pain, or if they cannot move the eye normally.</p>
<h4>Snow blindness</h4>
<p>Sunburn of the eye from sun reflecting off snow, water or sand. Painful, gritty, red, watery eyes, often hours later.</p>
<ul>
<li>Rest the eyes in the dark, with cool cloths over them.</li>
<li>It usually heals in 1-2 days.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent it:</strong> wear sunglasses. If you have none, make slit goggles from bark, cardboard or cloth with thin horizontal slits, and darken the skin under your eyes with charcoal.</li>
</ul><blockquote>Fallout is dust. Taking it off your body and keeping it out of your food and water removes most of the danger.</blockquote>
<h4>If you were outside during fallout</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Take off your outer clothing</strong> before you go in, or right away once inside. Do it gently so the dust doesn't blow around. This removes most of the contamination.</li>
<li>Put the clothes in a sealed plastic bag and leave it away from people and pets.</li>
<li><strong>Wash</strong>: shower or wash with soap, water and a gentle scrub. Wash your hair with shampoo.</li>
<li>Do not use conditioner, because it can bind dust to your hair.</li>
<li>Do not scrub hard or scratch your skin. Blow your nose, wipe your eyelids, and wipe your ears.</li>
<li>Put on clean clothes.</li>
<li>If you cannot shower, wipe your skin and hair with a clean damp cloth or wipes.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Pets</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wash them with soap and water, and keep them inside.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Food and water</h4>
<ul>
<li>Food in sealed containers, like cans and bottles, is safe once you wipe the outside.</li>
<li>Food in the fridge or freezer, or in closed packages, is safe.</li>
<li>Do not eat food that was outside and uncovered, or that may have dust on it.</li>
<li>Water in pipes, water heaters, and sealed bottles is safe to drink. Water that was open to the air outside may not be.</li>
<li>Do not drink from open sources until officials say it is safe.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Hurt or sick</h4>
<ul>
<li>Treat injuries as normal first aid. Being contaminated does not make a hurt person a danger to you. Clean the wound if you can.</li>
<li>Get urgent medical care when it is safe to go out.</li>
</ul><blockquote>Radiation from fallout fades fast. Follow official instructions on when to leave. If none are available, use these rules.</blockquote>
<h4>The 7-10 rule of thumb</h4>
<ul>
<li>Every time the time since the blast <strong>multiplies by 7</strong>, the radiation falls to <strong>one tenth</strong>.</li>
<li>After 7 hours it is about 1/10 of what it was at 1 hour.</li>
<li>After 49 hours (about 2 days) it is about 1/100.</li>
<li>After about 2 weeks it is about 1/1000.</li>
<li>It is a rough guide for fallout only. It does not replace official advice.</li>
</ul>
<h4>How long to stay</h4>
<ul>
<li>At least 24 hours, and longer if you can. The first day is the most dangerous.</li>
<li>If you must go out for essentials, keep it short. Cover your skin, nose and mouth, then follow the cleaning steps when you return.</li>
<li>Move only if officials tell you to, or if the building is damaged or on fire.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Potassium iodide (KI)</h4>
<ul>
<li>KI protects the thyroid from radioactive iodine. It does <strong>not</strong> protect from other radiation.</li>
<li>Take it only if public health officials tell you to. Too much can hurt you.</li>
<li>It does not replace sheltering.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Helping others</h4>
<ul>
<li>Check on neighbours who cannot move easily, if you can do it without going outside.</li>
<li>Share information from official sources, not rumours.</li>
</ul><p>Make the plan together, write it down and practice it. Kids who know what to do are calmer and safer.</p>
<h4>Choose</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Two meeting places:</strong> one near home (a tree, a neighbor's porch) and one outside your area (a library, a relative's house).</li>
<li><strong>An out-of-area contact</strong> everyone calls or texts when separated. Long-distance texts often get through when local calls do not.</li>
<li><strong>A family code word</strong> that only trusted people know. A kid should not go with anyone who cannot say it.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Teach kids</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Their full name, your name and your phone number.</strong> Practice it like a song.</li>
<li><strong>How to call 911</strong> (or your local emergency number), and what to say: their name, where they are and what is wrong.</li>
<li>How to stay put and wait for a trusted adult if they get separated.</li>
<li>That in a fire they must never hide: get out and stay out. See <a class="xl" data-id="House Fire: Get Out">House Fire: Get Out</a>.</li>
<li>To find a safe stranger in a crowd if lost: a person in uniform, someone with kids, or a store worker.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Contact cards</h4>
<ul>
<li>Put a <strong>card with your names, numbers, the out-of-area contact, allergies and medicines</strong> in each child's backpack and pocket.</li>
<li>For little ones, write a number in permanent marker on their arm or sew a tag in their clothes, if you are separated.</li>
</ul>
<h4>School and childcare</h4>
<ul>
<li>Learn their <strong>emergency and reunification plan</strong>: where kids go and how you pick them up.</li>
<li>Keep your contact details up to date, and name backup adults who may collect your child.</li>
<li>Do not rush to the school unless told to. Roads must stay clear for buses and responders.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Go-bag for kids</h4>
<ul>
<li>Water, snacks, a change of clothes, a warm layer and shoes</li>
<li>Comfort item, book, cards, coloring supplies</li>
<li>Medicines, copies of prescriptions and a doctor's number</li>
<li>For babies: diapers, formula, bottles. See <a class="xl" data-id="Caring for a Baby Without Power">Caring for a Baby Without Power</a>.</li>
<li>Flashlight and a whistle</li>
</ul>
<h4>Practice</h4>
<p>Twice a year, run a drill: get out of the house in the dark, meet at your meeting place, make a call. Discuss what went well.</p>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Home Emergencies and Power Outages">Home Emergencies and Power Outages</a>.</p><p>A fever is the body fighting an infection. How the child <strong>acts</strong> matters more than the number on the thermometer.</p>
<h4>When it is an emergency</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38 C (100.4 F) or higher.</strong> Get help right away, even if the baby looks well.</li>
<li>A baby 3 to 12 months with a fever of 39 C (102.2 F) or more, or any fever that lasts more than a day or two.</li>
<li>A child with fever <strong>and</strong> any of these:</li>
<li>Trouble breathing, or fast breathing that does not settle</li>
<li>A stiff neck, severe headache, or sensitivity to light</li>
<li>A rash that does not fade when pressed with a glass</li>
<li>Very sleepy, hard to wake, confused, or very floppy</li>
<li>A seizure. See <a class="xl" data-id="Seizures">Seizures</a>.</li>
<li>Signs of dehydration: no tears, dry mouth, no urine for 8 hours, sunken soft spot on a baby's head</li>
<li>A fever that lasts more than 3 days, or is above 40 C (104 F)</li>
</ul>
<h4>How to measure</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use a digital thermometer. Under the arm is easy but less exact. For a baby, a rectal reading is the most accurate.</li>
<li>Do not use a glass mercury thermometer.</li>
</ul>
<h4>What to do at home</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Offer fluids often.</strong> Small sips, or breast milk and formula for babies. See <a class="xl" data-id="Diarrhea, Vomiting and Rehydration">Diarrhea, Vomiting and Rehydration</a>.</li>
<li>Dress them lightly and keep the room comfortable. Do not bundle them up.</li>
<li>Let them rest.</li>
<li><strong>Medicine is for comfort, not to bring the number to normal.</strong> Use acetaminophen (paracetamol), or ibuprofen only if the child is older than 6 months. Follow the label by <strong>weight</strong>, use the dosing syringe, and do not give extra doses.</li>
<li><strong>Never give aspirin.</strong></li>
<li>A lukewarm sponge bath can help comfort. Do not use cold water, ice or alcohol rubs.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Febrile seizures</h4>
<p>Some young children have a short seizure with a fast-rising fever. It looks frightening, but is usually brief and harmless.</p>
<ul>
<li>Lay the child on their side on a soft surface and clear the area. Do not put anything in their mouth or hold them down.</li>
<li>Time it. <strong>Call for help if it lasts more than 5 minutes</strong>, the child does not wake up afterward, or it is their first one.</li>
</ul>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Children Are Different">Children Are Different</a>.</p><p><img src="/wiki/img/watch_method.jpg" alt="Watch method" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Northern Hemisphere</h4>
<ol>
<li>Hold the watch flat.</li>
<li>Point the <strong>hour hand</strong> at the sun.</li>
<li>Halfway between the hour hand and the <strong>12</strong> is <strong>south</strong>.</li>
<li>During daylight saving time, use the <strong>1</strong> instead of the 12.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Southern Hemisphere</h4>
<ol>
<li>Point the <strong>12</strong> at the sun.</li>
<li>Halfway between the 12 and the hour hand is <strong>north</strong>.</li>
<li>During daylight saving time, use the 1 instead of the 12.</li>
</ol>
<h4>No analog watch?</h4>
<p>Draw a clock face on paper or the ground with the hands showing the current time, and use it the same way.</p>
<blockquote>This method is a rough guide, off by 20 degrees or more in some places and times, and it does not work well near the equator. Check it against other methods.</blockquote><h4>Where to look</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Follow the land downhill.</strong> Water collects in valleys, gullies and low ground.</li>
<li><strong>Green vegetation</strong> such as willows, cottonwoods, reeds and cattails usually means water is near or just under the surface.</li>
<li><strong>Animal trails</strong> often lead to water, especially where several trails join and head downhill.</li>
<li><strong>Birds</strong> circling or flying low and straight at dawn and dusk are often heading to water.</li>
<li><strong>Insects:</strong> bees and flies usually stay within a few kilometers of water.</li>
<li><strong>Dry stream beds:</strong> dig on the outside of bends, where the last water would sit. If the sand is damp, dig a hole and wait for water to seep in.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Collecting water</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rain:</strong> spread a tarp, poncho or plastic sheet and funnel it into a container. Rainwater is the cleanest natural source.</li>
<li><strong>Dew:</strong> at dawn, wipe grass with a cloth and wring it out.</li>
<li><strong>Snow and ice:</strong> melt it first. Eating snow lowers your body temperature. Ice gives more water per pot than snow.</li>
<li><strong>Transpiration bag:</strong> tie a clear plastic bag over a leafy, non-poisonous branch in the sun. Water condenses inside.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Treat all water from the ground, streams, lakes, snow and dew before drinking. Even clear mountain water can carry parasites.</blockquote>
<h4>Water to avoid</h4>
<ul>
<li>Seawater and urine: they dehydrate you faster.</li>
<li>Water near mines, factories, farm runoff, or with dead animals nearby.</li>
<li>Water with oily film, strong smell or no living plants around it.</li>
</ul><p>Fire gives warmth, light, safe water, cooked food, a signal and a big morale boost. Make it before you need it badly.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/fire_triangle.jpg" alt="The fire triangle" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>What a fire needs</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heat</strong> to start it.</li>
<li><strong>Fuel</strong> to burn.</li>
<li><strong>Oxygen.</strong> Do not pack wood too tightly. A fire that is smothered goes out.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Three sizes of fuel</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/fire_materials.jpg" alt="Tinder, kindling and fuel" loading="lazy"></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tinder</strong> catches a spark or flame: dry grass, birch bark, cedar bark shredded fine, pine needles, cattail fluff, cotton, paper, wood shavings.</li>
<li><strong>Kindling</strong> carries the flame: dry twigs from match to pencil thickness.</li>
<li><strong>Fuel</strong> keeps it going: sticks and logs from thumb to wrist thickness, then larger.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Gather all three sizes <strong>before</strong> you strike a light. A new fire dies in the time it takes to go looking for more sticks.</blockquote>
<h4>Finding dry wood in wet weather</h4>
<ul>
<li>Snap test: dry wood breaks with a clean crack. If it bends, it is too wet.</li>
<li>Take dead branches still on standing trees, not from the ground.</li>
<li>Look under thick evergreens, inside rotten stumps and under overhangs.</li>
<li>Split wet sticks; the center is often dry.</li>
<li>Shave thin curls (feather sticks) from dry wood for kindling.</li>
<li>Resin-rich pine wood (fatwood) burns even when damp.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Building up</h4>
<p>Light the tinder, then add kindling a few pieces at a time. Add larger fuel only when the smaller sizes are burning strongly. Blow gently at the base if it struggles.</p><p><img src="/wiki/img/fire_lays.jpg" alt="Teepee and log cabin fire lays" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Teepee</h4>
<p>Put tinder in the center and lean kindling around it in a cone. Leave a gap on the windward side to light it. Burns hot and fast; good for starting and for boiling water.</p>
<h4>Log cabin</h4>
<p>Start with a small teepee, then stack pairs of sticks around it, each layer at right angles, like a cabin. Gives steady heat and a good bed of coals for cooking.</p>
<h4>Lean-to fire</h4>
<p>Push a green stick into the ground at an angle over the tinder, or lay one thick log on the windward side. Lean kindling against it. The log shelters the start from wind.</p>
<h4>Star fire</h4>
<p>Lay several logs like the spokes of a wheel with their ends meeting in the fire. Push them in as they burn. Saves fuel and lasts all night.</p>
<h4>Dakota fire hole</h4>
<p>Dig a hole about 30 cm (1 ft) deep and wide for the fire. Dig a second hole beside it, on the windward side, and join them with a tunnel at the bottom for air. Burns hot, uses little wood, is hard to see and handles wind well.</p>
<blockquote>In snow, build the fire on a platform of green logs or rocks so it does not melt into the snow and drown.</blockquote><h4>Choosing a site</h4>
<ul>
<li>Clear a 3 m (10 ft) circle down to bare soil. Remove leaves and dry grass.</li>
<li>Stay away from overhanging branches, tree roots, peat and dry grass.</li>
<li>Do not build against a large rock you want to sit by; wet rocks from rivers can crack or burst when heated.</li>
<li>Pay attention to wind. Sparks carry far in dry weather.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Carbon monoxide</h4>
<blockquote>Never burn a fire, stove, charcoal or generator inside a closed tent, car, snow cave or small room. Carbon monoxide has no smell and kills in your sleep.</blockquote>
<p>Signs of poisoning: headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion, sleepiness. Get the person into fresh air at once.</p>
<h4>Putting a fire out</h4>
<ol>
<li>Let it burn down to ash if you can.</li>
<li>Pour water on it and stir the ashes. Pour more and stir again.</li>
<li>If water is scarce, smother with soil or sand and stir, but do not just bury it; buried coals can stay hot for a day.</li>
<li>Feel for heat with the back of your hand held close. If it is too hot to touch, it is too hot to leave.</li>
</ol>
<h4>If a wildfire threatens</h4>
<ul>
<li>Move to areas with little fuel: rock, gravel, a recently burned area, or across a wide river.</li>
<li>Move downhill and across the slope, not uphill. Fire races uphill.</li>
<li>Cover your skin with wool or cotton, not synthetics, and breathe through a damp cloth.</li>
</ul><blockquote>This wiki is a field reference, not medical training. Take a first aid or wilderness first aid course. Get professional care as soon as you can.</blockquote>
<h4>1. Make the scene safe</h4>
<p>Check for traffic, fire, falling rocks, water, electricity or an animal. Do not become a second casualty.</p>
<h4>2. Check the person</h4>
<p>Tap their shoulder and shout. Are they awake and answering?</p>
<h4>3. Call or send for help</h4>
<p>If you have any signal, call your local emergency number (911 in North America, 112 in Europe and many countries). Text messages sometimes get through when calls do not.</p>
<h4>4. Work through the big killers, in order</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Massive bleeding:</strong> stop it first. See <a class="xl" data-id="Severe Bleeding">Severe Bleeding</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Airway:</strong> open it with head tilt and chin lift. Clear anything visible in the mouth.</li>
<li><strong>Breathing:</strong> look, listen and feel for up to 10 seconds. Gasping is not normal breathing.</li>
<li><strong>No normal breathing:</strong> start <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Circulation:</strong> look for other bleeding and signs of <a class="xl" data-id="Shock">Shock</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>5. Then check head to toe</h4>
<p>Look and gently feel for injuries: head, neck, chest, belly, pelvis, arms and legs. Ask what hurts. Note what you find and the time.</p>
<h4>Recovery position</h4>
<p>For someone breathing but not awake (and with no suspected spine injury): roll them onto their side, top knee bent to hold them there, head tilted back slightly so fluids drain from the mouth. Check breathing often.</p>
<h4>Protect them</h4>
<p>Keep them warm, off the cold ground, and calm. Do not give food or drink to someone who is badly injured or not fully alert.</p><p>Floods kill more people in vehicles than anywhere else. Water is stronger than it looks.</p>
<h4>Before the water comes</h4>
<ul>
<li>Know the high ground near you and two ways to reach it.</li>
<li>Move valuables and medicines up high. Keep a go-bag by the door.</li>
<li>If a flood warning is issued for your area, <strong>leave early</strong>. Do not wait for water to reach your door.</li>
</ul>
<h4>During a flood</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Move to higher ground right away.</strong> Do not wait for instructions if water is rising fast.</li>
<li><strong>Turn around, do not drown.</strong> Never walk or drive through floodwater.</li>
<li>15 cm (6 in) of moving water can knock an adult down.</li>
<li>30 cm (1 ft) can float many cars.</li>
<li>60 cm (2 ft) can carry away most vehicles, trucks included.</li>
<li>You cannot judge depth or what is under the surface. Roads may be washed out.</li>
<li>If your car stalls in rising water, <strong>leave it</strong> and get to higher ground. See <a class="xl" data-id="Car in Water">Car in Water</a>.</li>
<li>Stay out of basements and low areas. Do not touch electrical equipment if you are wet or standing in water.</li>
<li>Inside a building with rising water, go to the highest floor. Go on the roof only if you must, and signal for help. Do not climb into a closed attic you cannot get out of.</li>
</ul>
<h4>After a flood</h4>
<ul>
<li>Floodwater carries sewage, fuel and chemicals. Wash hands often and avoid contact with it.</li>
<li>Treat all tap and well water as unsafe until officials say otherwise. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making Water Safe to Drink">Making Water Safe to Drink</a>.</li>
<li>Do not turn the power on if it has been under water. Get it checked first.</li>
<li>Throw out food that touched floodwater, and cans that are swollen or dented.</li>
<li>Dry the building out fast, within a day or two, to limit mold. Wear gloves and a mask for cleanup.</li>
<li>Watch for snakes, sharp debris and damaged roads.</li>
</ul><p>Food is the last of the big survival needs. Most healthy people can go weeks without it. Hunger is unpleasant, but it is rarely what kills.</p>
<h4>Why food still matters</h4>
<ul>
<li>It keeps you warm, especially at night in cold weather.</li>
<li>It keeps your mind sharp and your mood up.</li>
<li>Long-term survival, past a few days, needs a food plan.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Rules of thumb</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not spend more energy finding food than it gives you.</strong> Chasing animals is usually a losing trade.</li>
<li>Eat high-energy foods you carried first, in small regular amounts.</li>
<li>If water is very short, eat little. Digestion, especially of protein, uses water.</li>
<li>Easy calories: insects, fish, shellfish from clean water, nuts, and plants you know with certainty.</li>
<li>Cook all wild meat, fish and shellfish thoroughly.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Best returns for little effort</h4>
<ol>
<li>Fishing with a line, hooks, or simple traps in streams</li>
<li>Insects and grubs</li>
<li>Familiar plants, nuts and berries you can identify with certainty</li>
<li>Trapping small animals (where it is legal and you know how)</li>
</ol>
<p>See also: <a class="xl" data-id="Foraging Plants Safely">Foraging Plants Safely</a></p><h4>Keep it closed</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not open the fridge or freezer</strong> more than you must.</li>
<li>A closed fridge stays cold for about <strong>4 hours</strong>.</li>
<li>A full freezer holds for about <strong>48 hours</strong>, a half-full one for about 24 hours.</li>
<li>Group food together and fill empty space with ice, frozen jugs or bags. Use a cooler with ice for the fridge items.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The 2-hour rule</h4>
<ul>
<li>Perishable food that has been above <strong>4 C (40 F)</strong> for more than <strong>2 hours</strong> (1 hour if above 32 C / 90 F) should be thrown away.</li>
<li>A thermometer in the fridge tells you the real temperature.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Throw away (fridge)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Meat, poultry, fish and eggs</li>
<li>Milk, soft cheese, yogurt, cream</li>
<li>Cooked leftovers, deli food and cut fruit or vegetables</li>
<li>Anything that smells wrong</li>
</ul>
<h4>Usually safe</h4>
<ul>
<li>Hard cheese, butter, margarine</li>
<li>Whole fruits and vegetables, peanut butter, jelly, ketchup, mustard</li>
<li>Unopened bottled drinks</li>
</ul>
<h4>Freezer</h4>
<ul>
<li>If food still has ice crystals or is cold to the touch (at or below 4 C / 40 F), it is usually safe to refreeze or cook.</li>
<li>If it has thawed completely and was warm for over 2 hours, throw away meat, poultry, fish and dairy.</li>
<li>Don't refreeze ice cream or other items that have melted.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cooking and eating</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cook thawed food promptly. Cook meat to a safe temperature (see the cooking guide).</li>
<li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out.</strong> You cannot tell by smell or taste.</li>
<li>Do not taste food to check.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Use up the freezer first. Eat fresh and refrigerated items early, then canned and dry goods.</blockquote><blockquote>Only eat a plant if you can identify it with <strong>100% certainty</strong>. Several deadly plants look like common edible ones.</blockquote>
<h4>Never eat</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wild mushrooms</strong>, unless you are an expert. Some deadly species look harmless, and symptoms can take a day to appear, too late to treat.</li>
<li>Plants that look like <strong>wild carrot, parsnip or parsley</strong> (white umbrella-shaped flower clusters). This family includes water hemlock and poison hemlock, among the deadliest plants anywhere.</li>
<li>Plants with milky or discolored sap (some exceptions exist, but avoid unless you know them).</li>
<li>Beans, bulbs or seeds inside pods, unless known.</li>
<li>Plants with an almond smell (can mean cyanide).</li>
<li>White and yellow berries (most are poisonous); about half of red berries are too.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Commonly known safe foods (where you know them)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cattail:</strong> young shoots, roots and pollen. Found in marshes.</li>
<li><strong>Dandelion:</strong> leaves, flowers and roots.</li>
<li><strong>Clover:</strong> leaves and flowers, best cooked.</li>
<li><strong>Acorns:</strong> must be shelled, crushed and soaked in several changes of water to leach out bitter tannins.</li>
<li><strong>Pine:</strong> inner bark and needles (tea). Avoid yew, which looks similar and is very toxic.</li>
<li><strong>Blackberries, raspberries, blueberries:</strong> aggregate and blue berries are among the safest.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The universal edibility test</h4>
<p>Military manuals describe a slow test (touching, holding in the mouth, then eating small amounts over 24+ hours). It takes a long time, does not detect every toxin, and <strong>does not work for mushrooms</strong>. Use it only in true long-term emergencies with no other option.</p>
<h4>Cooking helps</h4>
<p>Boiling breaks down some toxins and kills parasites. Throw away the water if a plant is bitter.</p><p>FRS and GMRS handhelds share the same 22 channels around 462 and 467 MHz. FRS radios need no license. GMRS has more power and repeaters and needs a license in the US.</p>
<h4>Using them</h4>
<ol>
<li>Turn on both radios and set the <strong>same channel</strong>.</li>
<li>Set the privacy code (sometimes called CTCSS or "sub-channel") to <strong>0 or off</strong> so you can hear everyone. Codes only filter what you hear. They do not make a call private.</li>
<li><strong>Press and hold</strong> the push-to-talk button, wait a second, speak in a normal voice with the radio a few centimeters from your mouth, then release.</li>
<li>Only one person can talk at a time. If you are talking, you cannot hear.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Channels</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Channels 1 to 7 and 15 to 22</strong> are the stronger channels. Channels 8 to 14 are low power, mainly for short-range use.</li>
<li><strong>Channel 9</strong> is widely used as an emergency and calling channel, and is monitored by some volunteer groups. Many families agree on a different channel for themselves.</li>
<li>Everyone nearby can hear you. Do not give out personal details.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Range</h4>
<ul>
<li>Usually 1 to 3 km (0.5 to 2 miles) in towns and woods. In open country, or from a hilltop, it can be much farther.</li>
<li>Buildings, hills and dense trees cut range a lot. See <a class="xl" data-id="Getting More Range from a Radio">Getting More Range</a>.</li>
<li>Packaging claims of "30 miles" are not realistic.</li>
</ul>
<h4>GMRS extras</h4>
<ul>
<li>Higher power and detachable antennas for much better range.</li>
<li><strong>Repeaters</strong> on channels 15R to 22R can cover a whole town. Repeater access needs a privacy code the repeater owner gives you.</li>
<li>Check your local rules. In the US, a GMRS license covers your whole family.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use short, clear messages. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making an Emergency Radio Call">Making an Emergency Radio Call</a>.</li>
<li>Agree on a check-in time. Turn radios on only at set times to save batteries.</li>
<li>Keep spare batteries or a charger. Rechargeable radios need a way to charge.</li>
<li>A car charger or power bank keeps radios alive when the power is out.</li>
</ul><p>Natural gas and propane can explode from a tiny spark. A gas company adds a smell, like <strong>rotten eggs or sulfur</strong>, so you notice a leak.</p>
<h4>If you smell gas or hear hissing</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Do not</strong> use light switches, appliances, garage door openers, lighters, matches or a phone indoors. Any spark can ignite gas.</li>
<li><strong>Do not</strong> start a car in an attached garage.</li>
<li><strong>Get everyone out</strong> right now, including pets. Leave doors open behind you if you can do so quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Move well away</strong> from the building, upwind.</li>
<li><strong>Call the gas company's emergency number and 911 from outside</strong> or from a neighbor's house.</li>
<li><strong>Do not go back in</strong> until the gas company or fire department says it is safe.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Shutting off the gas</h4>
<ul>
<li>Only do this if you can reach the main valve safely and <strong>without going into a gassy area</strong>.</li>
<li>Use a wrench. Turn the valve a quarter turn so the handle is <strong>crossways</strong> to the pipe.</li>
<li>Once it is off, <strong>do not turn it back on yourself.</strong> The gas company must restore service and relight pilot lights.</li>
<li>For propane tanks, close the valve on the tank by turning it clockwise.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Propane</h4>
<p>Propane is heavier than air. It sinks and collects in basements and low areas, so it can linger even with doors open.</p>
<h4>Signs of a leak</h4>
<ul>
<li>A sulfur or rotten egg smell, or a hissing or whistling sound near a gas line.</li>
<li>Dead or dying plants near a buried gas line, or bubbles in standing water.</li>
<li>Headaches, dizziness, nausea or fatigue indoors with no other cause. See <a class="xl" data-id="Carbon Monoxide">Carbon Monoxide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>After</h4>
<p>Do not use the appliance or relight anything until a professional inspects it.</p>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Utility Shutoffs">Utility Shutoffs</a>.</p><p>Portable generators are a leading cause of carbon monoxide deaths after storms.</p>
<h4>Where to run it</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Outdoors only</strong>, never in a garage, basement, shed, porch or near an open window or door.</li>
<li>Keep it at least <strong>6 m (20 ft)</strong> from the house, with the exhaust pointing away from any openings.</li>
<li>Put it on level ground in a dry spot. Keep it dry with a generator tent or canopy open on the sides. Never run it in rain or standing water.</li>
<li>Fit <strong>battery CO alarms</strong> in the house. See <a class="xl" data-id="Carbon Monoxide">Carbon Monoxide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Connecting it</h4>
<ul>
<li>Plug appliances <strong>directly into the generator</strong> or use a heavy-duty outdoor extension cord rated for the load. Check cords for damage.</li>
<li><strong>Never plug a generator into a wall outlet.</strong> This "backfeeding" can electrocute utility workers and neighbors and damage your house wiring.</li>
<li>To power the house wiring, have an electrician install a <strong>transfer switch</strong>.</li>
<li>Do not overload it. Add up the watts of what you plan to run, and start the biggest loads first.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Fuel</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Turn it off and let it cool</strong> before refueling. Gasoline spilled on a hot engine can ignite.</li>
<li>Store fuel in approved containers, outside, away from living spaces and ignition sources.</li>
<li>Do not store more fuel than you need. Fuel goes stale in a few months. Use a stabilizer.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Other tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use the generator only when needed and in short, planned periods.</li>
<li>Never touch it with wet hands.</li>
<li>Run it for a few minutes every month and check the oil. See <a class="xl" data-id="Home Emergencies and Power Outages">Home Emergencies and Power Outages</a>.</li>
</ul><p>Most handheld radios use line-of-sight signals. If you can see the other person, you can usually reach them. Anything in the way weakens the signal.</p>
<h4>Get high</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Height is the best range booster.</strong> Move to a hilltop, a rooftop, an upper floor or the top of a ridge.</li>
<li>A few meters of height can double your range.</li>
<li>Move out of a valley, a ravine or a basement.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Clear the path</h4>
<ul>
<li>Step away from buildings, vehicles, dense trees and cliffs.</li>
<li>Go to a window, or outdoors, when inside a building.</li>
<li>Metal, concrete and water absorb signals. A car roof can help as a reflector, but being inside a car blocks it a little.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Hold the radio right</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep the antenna <strong>straight up</strong>, not tilted or pressed against your head or body.</li>
<li>Keep your hand off the antenna. Do not hold it near your mouth.</li>
<li>Take the radio out of a pocket or pack while talking.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Better antennas</h4>
<ul>
<li>A longer, replacement antenna (often sold for handhelds) can improve range a bit.</li>
<li>A magnet-mount or roof antenna on a car, connected to a car radio, is much better than a handheld.</li>
<li>Do not transmit with no antenna attached. It can damage the radio.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Relay</h4>
<ul>
<li>A third person on high ground can <strong>relay</strong> messages between two who cannot hear each other.</li>
<li>In ham radio, repeaters do this automatically. See <a class="xl" data-id="Ham Radio in an Emergency">Ham Radio in an Emergency</a>.</li>
<li>On this device, every board you add extends the mesh. See <a class="xl" data-id="Using the Mesh Between Boards">Using the Mesh Between Boards</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Power and batteries</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cold batteries lose power quickly. Keep radios and spare batteries inside your coat.</li>
<li>Use low power for close calls. Switch to high power only when you need it.</li>
<li>Turn the radio off between calls, or use a scheduled check-in, to save the battery.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Test before you need it</h4>
<p>Try your radios at known distances and in different places. Write down what works.</p><p>Large symbols on the ground can be seen from aircraft kilometers away.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/ground_signals.jpg" alt="Ground-to-air signals" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>The key symbols</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>V</strong> - Need assistance</li>
<li><strong>X</strong> - Need medical assistance</li>
<li><strong>N</strong> - No</li>
<li><strong>Y</strong> - Yes</li>
<li><strong>Arrow</strong> - Going in this direction</li>
<li><strong>SOS</strong> - Distress (understood everywhere)</li>
</ul>
<h4>Making them visible</h4>
<ul>
<li>Make each symbol at least <strong>3 m (10 ft)</strong> long, with lines about 1 m (3 ft) wide. Bigger is better.</li>
<li>Use <strong>contrast</strong>: dark logs or rocks on snow, light stones or clothing on dark ground, trench lines in snow or sand so shadows show.</li>
<li>Make straight lines and sharp angles. Nature has few of them, so they stand out.</li>
<li>Pick an open area: a clearing, beach, ridge or frozen lake.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Answering aircraft with your body</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Both arms raised in a Y:</strong> Yes, we need help.</li>
<li><strong>One arm up, one arm down (diagonal N):</strong> No, we do not need help.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>If an aircraft rocks its wings or flashes lights, it has seen you.</blockquote><p>Amateur ("ham") radio has been the backup when phones, internet and power failed. Volunteer operators run emergency nets and relay messages to officials and hospitals.</p>
<h4>Licenses</h4>
<p>Transmitting on ham bands normally needs a license, which you get by passing a short exam. Licensed hams use call signs. Anyone can <strong>listen</strong> with no license.</p>
<p>In a <strong>real, life-threatening emergency</strong> with no other way to call for help, using any radio you have is widely accepted and almost never questioned. Learning and getting licensed before then is far better.</p>
<h4>The basics</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>VHF 2 meters (144 to 148 MHz):</strong> the most common handheld band.</li>
<li><strong>UHF 70 centimeters (420 to 450 MHz):</strong> also common on handhelds.</li>
<li><strong>Simplex:</strong> radio to radio, no repeater. Range is a few km on a handheld. <strong>146.520 MHz</strong> is the national 2 meter calling frequency. <strong>446.000 MHz</strong> is the 70 cm calling frequency.</li>
<li><strong>Repeaters:</strong> a tall antenna that re-transmits your signal, covering a town or region. You need its frequency, the offset and a tone, which local ham clubs publish.</li>
<li><strong>Nets:</strong> scheduled group calls. During disasters, emergency nets run on local repeaters.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Finding people</h4>
<ol>
<li>Tune to 146.520 MHz (simplex) and listen. Then tune local repeaters.</li>
<li>Listen first. If someone is already talking, wait.</li>
<li>Say your call sign or name, say you are monitoring, and ask if anyone is on frequency.</li>
<li>In a real emergency, say <strong>"Emergency traffic"</strong> or <strong>"Mayday"</strong> and give your message. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making an Emergency Radio Call">Making an Emergency Radio Call</a>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Etiquette in a disaster</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep calls short. Leave the channel free for emergency traffic.</li>
<li>Use plain language, not jargon, and say each message once clearly.</li>
<li>Write messages down and repeat them back.</li>
<li>Follow the instructions of the net control station.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Gear</h4>
<ul>
<li>A handheld (HT) with a spare battery and a better antenna than the stock "rubber duck".</li>
<li>A printed list of local repeaters and frequencies.</li>
<li>A way to charge: a car charger, power bank or small solar panel.</li>
</ul>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Getting More Range from a Radio">Getting More Range</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Choosing an Emergency Radio">Choosing an Emergency Radio</a>.</p><h4>When to wash</h4>
<ul>
<li>After using the toilet or changing a diaper</li>
<li>Before preparing or eating food</li>
<li>After handling raw meat, trash, animals or someone who is sick</li>
<li>After treating a wound or caring for the sick</li>
</ul>
<h4>The method (about 20 seconds)</h4>
<ol>
<li>Wet your hands. Pour a little water over them, away from the clean supply.</li>
<li>Lather with soap. Scrub palms, backs, between fingers and under nails for <strong>20 seconds</strong>.</li>
<li>Rinse with a small pour. Dry on a clean cloth or let the air dry them.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Soap and a quart of water is plenty.</strong> Soap does most of the work, not the amount of water.</p>
<h4>With very little water</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tippy tap:</strong> hang a jug from a string with a small hole near the cap. Tip it with a foot-string for a thin stream. Keep the soap on a string beside it.</li>
<li>Use a spray bottle or a cup with a hole poked in it.</li>
<li>Wash over a bucket and reuse the grey water to flush a toilet or water plants. Do not use it for drinking.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Hand sanitizer</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use one with <strong>at least 60% alcohol</strong>. Rub on all surfaces until dry (about 20 seconds).</li>
<li>It does not work well on visibly dirty or greasy hands. Wipe off dirt first.</li>
<li>It does not remove some germs, such as norovirus and C. diff. Soap and water is better after the toilet.</li>
</ul>
<h4>No soap</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use ash or clean sand to scrub, then rinse. It is better than water alone.</li>
<li>Scrub well and rinse with clean water.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Don't use bleach or harsh cleaners on skin. Save diluted bleach for surfaces.</blockquote><h4>After any blow to the head</h4>
<ul>
<li>If they were knocked out, even briefly, treat it as serious.</li>
<li>Assume a <strong>neck injury</strong> too if they fell from a height, were in a crash, or have neck pain, numbness or tingling. Keep the head and neck still. See <a class="xl" data-id="Broken Bones and Sprains">Broken Bones and Sprains</a>.</li>
<li>Control scalp bleeding with firm pressure. Scalps bleed a lot even from small cuts. Do not press hard if the skull feels soft or dented; press around the edges instead.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Concussion signs</h4>
<p>Headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion, blurred vision, ringing ears, memory gaps around the injury, feeling "foggy," trouble concentrating.</p>
<h4>Danger signs - get emergency help urgently</h4>
<ul>
<li>Getting <strong>more drowsy</strong> or hard to wake</li>
<li>Headache that gets <strong>worse</strong> and will not go away</li>
<li><strong>Repeated vomiting</strong></li>
<li>Confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech</li>
<li><strong>One pupil larger</strong> than the other</li>
<li>Weakness, numbness or trouble moving on one side</li>
<li><strong>Seizure</strong></li>
<li>Clear fluid or blood from the nose or ears</li>
<li>Bruising behind the ears or around both eyes</li>
</ul>
<h4>Caring for someone with a mild head injury</h4>
<ul>
<li>Rest. No hard physical work, and no more risk of another hit.</li>
<li>Have someone stay with them for at least the first 24 hours and check on them regularly. If they are asleep and cannot be woken normally, that is an emergency.</li>
<li>Avoid alcohol.</li>
<li>If unconscious but breathing, put them in the recovery position (on their side), supporting the head and neck.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Older adults and people on blood thinners can bleed inside the skull after even a minor bump. Signs may take hours or days. Watch them closely.</blockquote><h4>Heat exhaustion</h4>
<p><strong>Signs:</strong> heavy sweating, pale or flushed skin, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea, cramps, fast pulse.</p>
<ol>
<li>Move to shade and rest.</li>
<li>Loosen and remove extra clothing.</li>
<li>Cool with wet cloths and fanning.</li>
<li>Give water or a salt-and-sugar drink in small sips.</li>
<li>Rest for the rest of the day. If not better within an hour, treat as heat stroke.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Heat stroke (life-threatening)</h4>
<p><strong>Signs:</strong> very hot body, <strong>confusion, odd behavior, slurred speech, seizure or collapse</strong>. Skin may be dry or still sweating.</p>
<blockquote>Any confusion in a hot, overheated person is heat stroke until proven otherwise. Cool first, transport second.</blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Cool them <strong>immediately and aggressively</strong>.</li>
<li>Best: put them in cool or cold water up to the neck (a stream, lake or tub), keeping the head above water and staying with them.</li>
<li>If you cannot: soak them with water and fan hard. Put cold packs or wet cloths on the neck, armpits and groin. Keep re-wetting.</li>
<li>Keep cooling until they are thinking clearly again.</li>
<li>Do not give drinks to someone who is confused or not fully awake.</li>
<li>Get medical help. Heat stroke can damage organs even after recovery.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Prevention</h4>
<p>Drink regularly, rest in the shade, work in the cool hours, and watch each other for early signs.</p><blockquote>Heatstroke can kill within minutes. Cool first, then drive to a vet right after.</blockquote>
<p>Signs: heavy panting, thick drool, bright red gums, wobbling, vomiting, collapse.</p>
<h4>Do this now</h4>
<ul>
<li>Move the dog into shade or an air-conditioned space.</li>
<li>Wet the dog with cool (not ice-cold) water, especially the belly, armpits, and paws.</li>
<li>Fan the dog. Offer small sips of water. Do not force it.</li>
<li>Stop cooling once the dog is shivering or breathing settles. Overcooling is a risk too.</li>
<li>Go to a vet even if the dog seems better. Organ damage can show up later.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prevention</h4>
<ul>
<li>Never leave a dog in a parked car. Cracked windows do not help.</li>
<li>Walk in the cool of the day. Pavement burns paws long before it feels hot to you.</li>
<li>Flat-faced and heavy-coated dogs overheat fastest.</li>
</ul><p>Children watch adults to decide how scared to be. Your calm is the best comfort you can give.</p>
<h4>In the moment</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stay calm and speak slowly.</strong> Kneel to their level.</li>
<li>Tell them the truth in simple words: <em>"There is a big storm. We are going to the basement where we will be safe. I am with you."</em></li>
<li>Tell them what happens next, then do it. Predictability is calming.</li>
<li>Give them a small job: hold the flashlight, carry a teddy, pick the snacks. Feeling useful reduces fear.</li>
<li>Keep them close. Hold hands, hold them, or sit with them. Touch calms.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Comfort items</h4>
<p>Keep a comfort object, favorite book, small toy, cards or coloring things in your go-bag. Games like "I spy" and singing help pass the time and distract.</p>
<h4>Routines</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep meals, naps and bedtime as close to normal as you can.</li>
<li>Offer food and water often. Hungry, tired and thirsty kids are scared kids.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Limit the news</h4>
<p>Little ones do not need to see or hear frightening news and images. Turn off the TV or radio around them when you can.</p>
<h4>Listen</h4>
<ul>
<li>Let them talk, draw or play out what they feel. Do not say "do not be scared." Say "It is okay to be scared. I am here."</li>
<li>Answer questions simply and honestly. It is fine to say, "I do not know, but I will find out."</li>
<li>Do not promise what you cannot control. Say "We will do everything we can to stay safe."</li>
</ul>
<h4>Signs they are struggling</h4>
<ul>
<li>Clinging, crying, tantrums, going back to earlier behaviors (bed-wetting, thumb-sucking)</li>
<li>Trouble sleeping, nightmares, headaches, stomachaches</li>
<li>Fear of being alone, or of the sound of wind, thunder or sirens</li>
<li>Withdrawal, anger, or no interest in play</li>
</ul>
<p>These are common and usually settle over days to weeks with comfort and routine. If they last, or you are worried, ask a doctor, school counselor or crisis line.</p>
<h4>Look after yourself too</h4>
<p>You cannot pour from an empty cup. Eat, drink, rest when you can, and ask other adults to take a turn.</p>
<p>See <a class="xl" data-id="Staying Calm and Keeping Morale">Staying Calm and Keeping Morale</a>.</p><p>Search teams work in a systematic way. Help them by staying put, being visible, and making noise.</p>
<h4>Stay in one place</h4>
<p>Searchers look hardest around your last known location and route. A moving person is much harder to find, and may walk out of an area that was already searched.</p>
<h4>Be easy to see</h4>
<ul>
<li>Go to the most open spot nearby: a clearing, ridge, meadow, beach or riverbank.</li>
<li>Spread bright clothing, tarps or space blankets in the open.</li>
<li>Build <a class="xl" data-id="Ground-to-Air Signals">Ground-to-Air Signals</a> and keep a signal fire ready.</li>
<li>Use a <a class="xl" data-id="Signal Mirror">Signal Mirror</a> on any aircraft you see.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Be easy to hear</h4>
<ul>
<li>Blow your whistle in sets of three, then pause and listen.</li>
<li>Bang metal or rocks if you have no whistle.</li>
<li><strong>Answer</strong> when you hear your name called. Some lost people hide from searchers out of fear or embarrassment. Do not.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Leave clues if you move</h4>
<p>If you must move, leave a note at your last spot with the time, your direction and your condition. Mark your route with arrows and broken branches.</p>
<h4>When a helicopter comes</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stay in the open and wave both arms in a <strong>Y</strong> (yes, we need help).</li>
<li>Do not approach a helicopter unless the crew signals you to. Come from the <strong>front</strong>, where the pilot can see you, crouching low.</li>
<li>Never go near the tail rotor. Never approach from uphill, where blades are closer to the ground.</li>
<li>Tie down or hold onto loose items like tarps, hats and sleeping bags; the downdraft is very strong.</li>
<li>If you can, clear a landing area about 30 x 30 m (100 x 100 ft), flat, with no wires, tall trees or loose debris.</li>
</ul>
<h4>When searchers reach you</h4>
<p>Tell them: how many people, injuries, medical conditions, and anyone still missing.</p><p>Most emergencies happen at home: storms, floods, winter outages, wildfires. A little planning makes a huge difference.</p>
<h4>Family emergency plan</h4>
<ul>
<li>Pick two meeting places: one near home, one outside your neighborhood.</li>
<li>Choose an out-of-area contact person everyone calls or texts. Long-distance lines often work when local ones are jammed.</li>
<li>Write down important phone numbers on paper. Phones die.</li>
<li>Know how to turn off your water, gas and electricity.</li>
<li>Plan for pets, older family members and anyone with medical needs.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Home supplies (at least 3 days, ideally 2 weeks)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Water:</strong> about 4 liters (1 gallon) per person per day. See <a class="xl" data-id="Storing Water at Home">Storing Water at Home</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Food</strong> that needs no cooking or little water. See <a class="xl" data-id="Emergency Food Storage">Emergency Food Storage</a>.</li>
<li>Flashlights, headlamps and spare batteries (avoid candles if you can)</li>
<li>A battery or hand-crank radio</li>
<li>First aid kit and at least a week of any prescription medicines</li>
<li>Phone power banks</li>
<li>Warm clothing, blankets or sleeping bags</li>
<li>Cash in small bills</li>
<li>Copies of important documents in a waterproof bag</li>
</ul>
<h4>During a power outage</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep the fridge and freezer <strong>closed</strong>. A closed fridge stays cold for about 4 hours; a full freezer about 48 hours (24 if half full).</li>
<li>Unplug electronics to protect them from a surge when power returns. Leave one light switched on so you know when it is back.</li>
<li>In cold weather, close off unused rooms, gather in one room, cover windows with blankets and wear layers.</li>
<li>In hot weather, stay in the lowest, shadiest room and drink plenty of water.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><strong>Generators, grills and camp stoves must never be used indoors</strong>, including in garages, basements and on porches. Run generators at least 6 m (20 ft) from any window, door or vent. Fit a battery carbon monoxide alarm.</blockquote>
<h4>Food safety after an outage</h4>
<p>Throw out perishable food (meat, fish, dairy, eggs, leftovers) that has been above 4 C (40 F) for more than 2 hours. <strong>When in doubt, throw it out.</strong> Never taste food to check it.</p>
<h4>If told to evacuate</h4>
<p>Go early. Take your emergency kit, medicines, documents, phone chargers and pets. Follow the routes officials give; shortcuts may be blocked or dangerous.</p><p>Fire can fill a house with deadly smoke in two or three minutes. <strong>Get out first and call for help from outside.</strong></p>
<h4>If the alarm sounds or you see fire</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get out.</strong> Do not stop for belongings, phones or pets.</li>
<li><strong>Crawl low</strong> under smoke, where the air is cleaner.</li>
<li>Before opening a door, <strong>feel it with the back of your hand.</strong> If it is hot, do not open it. Use another way out.</li>
<li>Close doors behind you to slow the fire.</li>
<li>Go to your family's meeting place. Count everyone.</li>
<li><strong>Call 911 from outside.</strong> Never go back in. Tell firefighters if anyone is missing.</li>
</ol>
<h4>If you are trapped</h4>
<ul>
<li>Close the door and stuff towels or clothes (wet if possible) in the cracks. Cover vents.</li>
<li>Go to a window. Open it a little, or break it if you have to, and call or wave a bright cloth.</li>
<li>Call 911 and say exactly which room you are in.</li>
<li>Stay low near the window.</li>
<li>If your clothes catch fire, <strong>stop, drop and roll.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Burns">Burns</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Smoke alarms and plans</h4>
<ul>
<li>Put an alarm on every level and outside every sleeping area. Test monthly and replace batteries yearly. Replace alarms every 10 years.</li>
<li>Make a home escape plan with <strong>two ways out of every room</strong>. Practice it twice a year, including in the dark.</li>
<li>Fit a ladder in upper bedrooms if you have no other escape.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Kitchen fires</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grease fire:</strong> turn off the heat and slide a lid over the pan. <strong>Never use water.</strong> Baking soda or salt can smother a small one.</li>
<li>Oven fire: keep the door closed and turn the oven off.</li>
<li>Never carry a burning pan outside.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Small fires and extinguishers</h4>
<p>Use one only if the fire is small, you have a clear exit behind you and the room is not filling with smoke. Remember <strong>PASS</strong>: Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side. If in doubt, <strong>get out.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Fire Safety">Fire Safety</a>.</p><p><strong>Storm surge</strong> (the wall of seawater pushed ashore) is the deadliest part of a hurricane. Wind is second.</p>
<h4>Before the storm</h4>
<ul>
<li>Know if you live in an evacuation zone. <strong>If officials say evacuate, go.</strong> Leave early to beat traffic and flooded roads.</li>
<li>Fill the car with fuel and withdraw some cash. Take medicines, documents, phone chargers, pets and a go-bag.</li>
<li>Fill the bathtub and containers with water. See <a class="xl" data-id="Storing Water at Home">Storing Water at Home</a>.</li>
<li>Bring in or tie down anything the wind can pick up. Board up or shutter windows if you can.</li>
<li>Freeze water bottles to keep the fridge cold longer.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you stay (outside surge zones only)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Pick a small interior room on the lowest level that will not flood. A closet or bathroom with no windows is best.</li>
<li>Stay away from windows and glass doors. Cover yourself with mattresses or blankets.</li>
<li>Have a radio, flashlight, first aid kit and water with you.</li>
<li><strong>The calm eye is not the end.</strong> Winds return from the other direction, often fiercely. Stay inside until officials say it is over.</li>
<li>If water rises in your home, go to the highest floor. Do not retreat into a closed attic.</li>
</ul>
<h4>After the storm</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stay out of floodwater. See <a class="xl" data-id="Floods and Flash Floods">Floods and Flash Floods</a>.</li>
<li>Do not drive through standing water or over damaged roads and bridges.</li>
<li>Treat every downed line as live. See <a class="xl" data-id="Downed Power Lines">Downed Power Lines</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Never run a generator, grill or camp stove indoors.</strong> Carbon monoxide poisoning kills many people after storms. See <a class="xl" data-id="Generator Safety">Generator Safety</a>.</li>
<li>Check on neighbors, especially older people. Document damage with photos before cleanup.</li>
</ul><p>Hypothermia can happen well above freezing when someone is wet, tired and in the wind.</p>
<h4>Signs - watch for the "umbles"</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mild:</strong> shivering, stumbling, fumbling hands, mumbling, poor decisions.</li>
<li><strong>Moderate to severe:</strong> shivering slows or stops, confusion, drowsiness, slurred speech, acting strangely (some people even undress).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Treatment</h4>
<ol>
<li>Get them out of the wind and wet. Shelter first.</li>
<li>Replace wet clothes with dry ones, or cover wet clothes with a waterproof layer if you cannot change them.</li>
<li>Insulate from the ground, and wrap the whole body, including the head and neck.</li>
<li>Put warm (not hot) water bottles or heat packs, wrapped in cloth, on the chest, armpits and back.</li>
<li>If fully awake and able to swallow: give warm, sweet drinks and food.</li>
<li>Share body heat in a sleeping bag if needed.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Handle a severely cold person very <strong>gently</strong> and keep them lying flat. Rough movement can trigger a fatal heart rhythm. Do not rub the skin. No alcohol.</blockquote>
<p>If they are unresponsive and not breathing normally after checking for up to a full minute, start <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a>.</p>
<h4>Frostbite</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Signs:</strong> numb, white or grey-yellow skin that feels hard or waxy. Fingers, toes, nose, ears.</li>
<li>Do not rub it, and do not use direct heat like a fire or stove.</li>
<li><strong>Only rewarm if it will not freeze again.</strong> Refreezing does much more damage.</li>
<li>Rewarm in warm water about 37-39 C (99-102 F), comfortable to your own hand, for 15-30 minutes. It will be very painful.</li>
<li>Do not pop blisters. Cover loosely with dry, clean dressing and put gauze between fingers and toes.</li>
</ul><h4>Is the ice safe?</h4>
<p>Clear, hard, new ice is strongest. White or cloudy ice is weaker, often about half as strong.</p>
<ul>
<li>Under 5 cm (2 in): <strong>stay off</strong>.</li>
<li>10 cm (4 in) of clear ice: walking for one person.</li>
<li>13-18 cm (5-7 in): a snowmobile or small all-terrain vehicle.</li>
<li>20-30 cm (8-12 in): a small car.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ice is never the same thickness everywhere. It is thinner near inlets, outlets, moving water, docks, rocks, logs and dark spots, and under snow. Check thickness every few steps with an ice chisel, axe or auger.</p>
<blockquote>Never go onto ice alone. Carry ice picks (two spikes on a cord around your neck) and a throw rope.</blockquote>
<h4>If you fall in: the 1-10-1 rule</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/ice_1101.jpg" alt="The 1-10-1 rule for falling through ice" loading="lazy"></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>1 minute:</strong> cold shock makes you gasp and breathe fast. Keep your head above water, do not panic, and slow your breathing. It passes.</li>
<li><strong>10 minutes:</strong> of useful movement before your arms and legs go numb. Use them to get out now.</li>
<li><strong>1 hour:</strong> before you lose consciousness from hypothermia, even if you cannot get out. Keep your arms on the ice; they may freeze to it and hold you up.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Getting out</h4>
<ol>
<li>Turn toward the direction you came from. That ice held you before.</li>
<li>Put your arms flat on the ice. Use ice picks, keys or a knife to grip.</li>
<li><strong>Kick your legs hard</strong> to bring your body up level with the surface, like a swimmer.</li>
<li>Pull and kick until your chest and hips are on the ice.</li>
<li><strong>Roll away</strong> from the hole. Do not stand up until you are on thick ice.</li>
<li>Get to shelter and warmth immediately. See <a class="xl" data-id="Hypothermia and Frostbite">Hypothermia and Frostbite</a>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Helping someone else</h4>
<p><strong>Reach, throw, don't go.</strong> Do not run out to them; you will likely fall in too.</p>
<ul>
<li>Call for help.</li>
<li>Reach with a long pole, branch, rope or ladder while lying flat or standing on solid ice or shore.</li>
<li>Throw a rope or anything that floats.</li>
<li>If you must go out, lie flat to spread your weight and push something ahead of you for them to grab.</li>
</ul><p>A layered filter removes mud, debris and some taste. It does <strong>not</strong> make water safe on its own. Always boil or disinfect after filtering.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/water_filter.jpg" alt="Layers of an improvised filter" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>What you need</h4>
<ul>
<li>A container with a small hole in the bottom: plastic bottle with the bottom cut off, a bucket, or a cloth tied into a cone</li>
<li>Cloth or grass</li>
<li>Fine sand</li>
<li>Charcoal from your fire (not ash), crushed into small pieces</li>
<li>Small gravel or pebbles</li>
</ul>
<h4>Building it</h4>
<ol>
<li>If using a bottle, cut off the bottom and turn it upside down. Plug the neck loosely with cloth.</li>
<li>Add layers from the bottom up: cloth, small gravel, fine sand, crushed charcoal, fine sand, then cloth or grass on top.</li>
<li>Make each layer several centimeters thick. More sand and charcoal means clearer water.</li>
<li>Pour water in slowly. Throw away the first batch; it will carry dust from the charcoal.</li>
<li>Run cloudy water through two or three times if needed.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Charcoal from a campfire is not the same as activated charcoal. It helps with taste and color, but it does not remove chemicals or germs reliably.</blockquote>
<h4>After filtering</h4>
<p>Boil, use bleach, or use tablets. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making Water Safe to Drink">Making Water Safe to Drink</a>.</p><h4>Insects</h4>
<p>Insects are high in protein and fat and easy to catch.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Good choices:</strong> grasshoppers, crickets, ants, termites, beetle grubs (in rotten logs), earthworms.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid:</strong> brightly colored insects, hairy caterpillars, anything that stings or bites (except ants once cooked), flies, ticks and mosquitoes.</li>
<li>Cook them: roast or boil. Remove legs and wings from grasshoppers. Cooking kills parasites.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Fishing</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Improvised hooks:</strong> bent safety pins, wire, thorns, carved bone or hardwood.</li>
<li><strong>Line:</strong> fishing line from your kit, inner strands of paracord, dental floss.</li>
<li><strong>Bait:</strong> insects, worms, small pieces of fish guts.</li>
<li>Fish in the early morning and evening, in shade, near logs and in deeper pools below rapids.</li>
<li>Set several lines at once and check them often, rather than holding one rod.</li>
<li>A <strong>fish trap</strong>: a ring of stones in shallow water with a narrow funnel entrance. Fish swim in and struggle to find their way out.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Cook all freshwater fish thoroughly. Avoid fish that look sick, smell bad, or have slimy gills or sunken eyes.</blockquote>
<h4>Shellfish</h4>
<ul>
<li>Freshwater mussels and crayfish are good, cooked well.</li>
<li>Seashore shellfish can carry toxins from algae blooms (red tide), which cooking does not destroy. Avoid during warnings or in warm months if unsure.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Small game</h4>
<p>Snares and traps work, but take skill and time, and are illegal in many places outside emergencies. Check many traps over a wide area. Always cook wild meat until it is no longer pink.</p><p>A battery that clicks or turns slowly and weakly is usually just flat. Check your car manual first. Some modern cars have special jump points.</p>
<h4>Before you start</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do <strong>not</strong> try if the battery is cracked, leaking, bulging or frozen.</li>
<li>Switch both cars off and take the keys out. Turn off lights and radio.</li>
<li>No smoking, flames or sparks near batteries. They give off explosive gas.</li>
<li>Park the cars close but <strong>not touching</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Connect the cables in this order</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Red</strong> clamp to the <strong>positive (+)</strong> terminal of the <strong>dead</strong> battery.</li>
<li>Other <strong>red</strong> clamp to the <strong>positive (+)</strong> terminal of the <strong>good</strong> battery.</li>
<li><strong>Black</strong> clamp to the <strong>negative (-)</strong> terminal of the <strong>good</strong> battery.</li>
<li>Last <strong>black</strong> clamp to <strong>bare, unpainted metal</strong> on the dead car's engine block or frame, <strong>away from the battery</strong>. This is the ground. It may spark a little; that is why it goes away from the battery.</li>
</ol>
<p>Make sure the clamps do not touch each other.</p>
<h4>Start</h4>
<ol>
<li>Start the <strong>good</strong> car and let it run a few minutes.</li>
<li>Try to start the dead car. If it does not start, wait a few more minutes and try again. Do not crank for more than about 10 seconds at a time.</li>
<li>Once it starts, leave it running.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Remove the cables in reverse order</h4>
<p>Black from the ground, black from the good battery, red from the good battery, red from the dead battery.</p>
<h4>After</h4>
<ul>
<li>Drive for at least 20 to 30 minutes to recharge the battery. Do not turn the engine off during this time.</li>
<li>If it keeps dying, the battery or alternator may be failing. Get it tested.</li>
<li>In the cold, a battery loses power. A portable jump pack is a good addition to your kit. See <a class="xl" data-id="Car Emergency Kit">Car Emergency Kit</a>.</li>
</ul><h4>Keep animals away</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cook and eat at least 60 m (200 ft) downwind from where you sleep.</li>
<li>Hang food, trash and anything with a smell (toothpaste, soap) from a branch at least 4 m (12 ft) up and 1.5 m (5 ft) out from the trunk, or use a bear canister.</li>
<li>Never keep food in your shelter in bear country.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Avoid food poisoning</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wash hands with soap, or use sanitizer, before handling food.</li>
<li>Boil water used for cooking and washing dishes.</li>
<li>Eat cooked food right away. In warm weather, cooked food left out for more than 2 hours can make you sick.</li>
<li>Throw away cans that bulge, leak, spurt when opened, or smell bad.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Preserving meat and fish</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drying:</strong> cut into thin strips, no thicker than 6 mm (1/4 in), and dry in sun and wind or over a low, smoky fire until brittle.</li>
<li><strong>Smoking:</strong> keep strips in heavy smoke over a low fire for many hours. Smoke keeps insects off and helps preserve.</li>
<li><strong>Cold:</strong> in winter, freeze meat outside in a shaded, animal-proof place.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Thin strips and complete drying are key. Thick or damp meat spoils quickly.</blockquote><p>A lean-to is quick to build and pairs well with a fire in front. It blocks wind and rain from one side.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/lean_to.jpg" alt="Lean-to with fire reflector" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Building it</h4>
<ol>
<li>Find two trees about 2-3 m apart, or drive two forked sticks into the ground.</li>
<li>Tie or wedge a strong <strong>ridge pole</strong> across them at about chest height.</li>
<li>Lean long poles against the ridge pole on the windward side, close together, at about 45-60 degrees.</li>
<li>Weave smaller branches across the poles.</li>
<li>Cover with layers of bark, leafy branches, grass or a tarp, starting at the bottom so each layer overlaps the one below, like shingles.</li>
<li>Make a thick bed of dry insulation inside.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Adding a fire reflector</h4>
<p>Build a fire about one step in front of the open side. Behind the fire, stack green logs between stakes to make a wall. It throws heat back into the shelter.</p>
<blockquote>The back of the lean-to must face the wind. Check the wind direction before you start, and watch for changes.</blockquote><h4>Lightning</h4>
<ul>
<li>If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck. Get to a building or hard-topped vehicle if you can.</li>
<li>Avoid summits, ridges, open fields, lone trees, water and metal fences.</li>
<li>In a forest, go to a low area among smaller trees of similar height.</li>
<li>Groups should spread out at least 15 m (50 ft) apart so one strike does not hit everyone.</li>
<li>Wait 30 minutes after the last thunder before going back out.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>There is no truly safe place outdoors in a thunderstorm. If caught in the open with no option, crouch low on the balls of your feet, feet together, away from tall objects.</blockquote>
<h4>Flash floods</h4>
<ul>
<li>Never camp in a dry stream bed or narrow canyon.</li>
<li>Rain far upstream can cause a flood where the sky is clear.</li>
<li>Do not walk or drive through moving water. 15 cm (6 in) of fast water can knock you down; 60 cm (2 ft) can float a car.</li>
<li>Climb to higher ground if you hear a roar or see water rising and muddying.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Wind and cold fronts</h4>
<ul>
<li>A sudden temperature drop, shifting wind and dark clouds mean bad weather is near. Get shelter set up early.</li>
<li>Wind steals body heat fast. Block the wind before anything else.</li>
</ul><h4>If a child goes missing</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Act at once.</strong> Do not wait to see if they come back.</li>
<li><strong>Check water first.</strong> Pools, ponds, buckets, bathtubs, streams and ditches are the biggest danger for young children.</li>
<li><strong>Check hiding places:</strong> closets, cars (including trunks), under beds, sheds, behind furniture.</li>
<li><strong>Call out</strong> and listen. Ask everyone nearby to help.</li>
<li><strong>Call 911 (or your local number) early.</strong> A fast call is better than a late one. Give a <strong>description, clothing, age, where and when last seen, and a photo</strong> if you have it.</li>
<li>Leave <strong>one person at the last-seen spot</strong> in case the child returns.</li>
<li>In the outdoors, search along <strong>trails, roads and the direction downhill or downwind</strong>, and mark places already checked.</li>
<li>Keep calling. Blow a whistle in 3 blasts. Shut off noise sometimes to listen.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Children who wander</h4>
<p>Some children, such as those with autism, tend to wander. Fit a door chime, lock doors high up, tell neighbors, and <strong>search water first</strong> every time.</p>
<h4>Teach kids if they are lost outdoors: "Hug a Tree"</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stop and stay put.</strong> Do not wander. A child who stays in one place is found much sooner.</li>
<li><strong>Hug a tree</strong>, or sit next to a tree or rock. It is a friend while you wait.</li>
<li><strong>Make noise.</strong> Blow a whistle (3 blasts), and shout for help in short bursts.</li>
<li><strong>Stay dry and warm.</strong> Put on a jacket. Make a bed of leaves, or cover yourself with a trash bag or leaves.</li>
<li><strong>Do not hide</strong> from searchers. They will call your name.</li>
<li><strong>Do not go in water,</strong> and do not follow a stream.</li>
</ul>
<p>Give every child a <strong>whistle</strong> and a bright jacket or hat for hikes. Tell them to stay on the trail and to stop when they cannot see you.</p>
<h4>In a crowd or store</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stop where you are and call out their name.</li>
<li>Teach kids to find a person in uniform or a store worker, or a mom with kids, and say "I am lost."</li>
<li>Ask the staff to lock the exits and call the police.</li>
</ul>
<h4>When found</h4>
<p>Hold them, give water and food, check for injuries and cold or heat illness. See <a class="xl" data-id="Hypothermia and Frostbite">Hypothermia and Frostbite</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Heat Exhaustion and Heat Stroke">Heat Exhaustion and Heat Stroke</a>. <strong>Do not scold.</strong> Praise them for staying put.</p>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Helping Searchers Find You">Helping Searchers Find You</a>.</p><h4>Step 1: listen</h4>
<p>Listen for a few seconds. If someone is already talking about an emergency, wait.</p>
<h4>Step 2: call for help</h4>
<p>Press the transmit button, wait a second, then speak slowly and clearly:</p>
<blockquote><strong>"Mayday, mayday, mayday.</strong> This is [your name or call sign]. [Repeat once.] I am at [location]. I have [what is wrong]. [Number of people]. I need [what you need]. <strong>Over.</strong>"</blockquote>
<p>Example: <em>"Mayday, mayday, mayday. This is Alex. I am on Ridge Road, 2 km south of the old bridge. My car is in a ditch and one person has a broken leg. Two people. I need an ambulance. Over."</em></p>
<p>Use <strong>"Mayday"</strong> only for danger to life. For less serious urgent calls say <strong>"Pan-pan"</strong> (pronounced "pahn-pahn") three times.</p>
<h4>Step 3: wait and repeat</h4>
<ul>
<li>Release the button and listen. If nobody answers, wait about one minute, then try again.</li>
<li>Try other channels or frequencies if you can. See <a class="xl" data-id="FRS and GMRS Walkie-Talkies">FRS and GMRS Walkie-Talkies</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Ham Radio in an Emergency">Ham Radio in an Emergency</a>.</li>
<li>Keep saying your location. Rescuers need it.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Say these things</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Who</strong> you are, and how many people.</li>
<li><strong>Where</strong> you are (road, mile marker, landmark, coordinates).</li>
<li><strong>What</strong> is wrong.</li>
<li><strong>What help</strong> you need.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Useful radio words</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Over:</strong> I am finished and waiting for your reply.</li>
<li><strong>Out:</strong> this conversation is finished. Do not say "over and out" together.</li>
<li><strong>Roger:</strong> I received your message.</li>
<li><strong>Say again:</strong> repeat your last message.</li>
<li><strong>Stand by:</strong> wait.</li>
<li><strong>Affirmative / negative:</strong> yes / no.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Phonetic alphabet</h4>
<p>Alfa, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, Golf, Hotel, India, Juliett, Kilo, Lima, Mike, November, Oscar, Papa, Quebec, Romeo, Sierra, Tango, Uniform, Victor, Whiskey, X-ray, Yankee, Zulu.</p>
<p>Say numbers one at a time: "four five two" for 452. Say "niner" for 9 so it is not confused with "five".</p>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Speak slowly, with the radio a few centimeters from your mouth.</li>
<li>Keep messages short. Save the battery.</li>
<li>After rescue, tell the net or channel that the emergency is over.</li>
</ul>
<p>See also <a class="xl" data-id="Radio and Phone Tips">Radio and Phone Tips</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Helping Searchers Find You">Helping Searchers Find You</a>.</p><p>Clear water can still make you very sick. Use one of these methods. If the water is cloudy, let it settle and pour it through cloth first.</p>
<h4>Boiling (most reliable)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Bring water to a <strong>rolling boil for 1 minute</strong>.</li>
<li>Above 2,000 m (6,500 ft), boil for <strong>3 minutes</strong>.</li>
<li>Let it cool covered. Boiling kills germs but does not remove chemicals.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Household bleach</h4>
<p>Use plain, unscented bleach (no added cleaners or scent). Check the strength on the label.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>6% bleach:</strong> 8 drops per 4 liters (1 gallon), 2 drops per liter</li>
<li><strong>8.25% bleach:</strong> 6 drops per 4 liters (1 gallon), 2 drops per liter</li>
<li>If the water is cloudy or very cold, <strong>double</strong> the dose.</li>
<li>Stir and wait <strong>30 minutes</strong>. It should smell slightly of chlorine. If not, repeat the dose and wait 15 more minutes.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Tablets and drops</h4>
<p>Chlorine dioxide, iodine or chlorine tablets work well. Follow the package exactly, especially the waiting time. Chlorine dioxide also kills tough parasites like Cryptosporidium if you wait the full time (often 4 hours).</p>
<blockquote>Iodine is not suitable for pregnant women, people with thyroid problems, or for use over many weeks.</blockquote>
<h4>Filters</h4>
<p>A good pump or squeeze filter removes bacteria and parasites. Most do not stop viruses. In doubt, filter and then disinfect.</p>
<h4>Sunlight (SODIS)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Fill clear plastic (PET) bottles, 2 liters or smaller, with clear water.</li>
<li>Lay them in full sun for <strong>6 hours</strong>, or <strong>2 days</strong> if it is cloudy.</li>
<li>Does not work with cloudy water or in rain.</li>
</ul>
<p>See also: <a class="xl" data-id="Improvised Gravity Filter">Improvised Gravity Filter</a></p><h4>Orient the map</h4>
<ol>
<li>Lay the compass on the map with its edge along a north-south grid line.</li>
<li>Turn the map and compass together until the red needle points to north on the map (allowing for declination, below).</li>
<li>The map now matches the land around you.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Take a bearing to a landmark</h4>
<ol>
<li>Point the travel arrow on the compass at the landmark.</li>
<li>Turn the dial until the orienting arrow lines up under the red needle ("red in the shed").</li>
<li>Read the bearing at the index line.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Follow a bearing</h4>
<ol>
<li>Hold the compass flat in front of you, travel arrow forward.</li>
<li>Turn your body until the needle sits in the orienting arrow.</li>
<li>Pick a tree or rock on that line, walk to it, then repeat.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Declination</h4>
<p>Compass north (magnetic north) differs from map north. The difference, printed on most maps, can be 10-20 degrees or more in some regions. Adjust for it, or you will drift far off course over distance.</p>
<h4>Other tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep metal, phones and magnets away from the compass.</li>
<li><strong>Aiming off:</strong> if heading to a point on a river or road, aim deliberately to one side, so you know which way to turn when you reach it.</li>
<li><strong>Handrails:</strong> follow long features like rivers, ridges or trails.</li>
<li><strong>Count paces</strong> to estimate distance: most people take about 60-70 double paces per 100 m on flat ground.</li>
</ul><h4>How much daylight is left?</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/hand_sunset.jpg" alt="Estimating time until sunset with your hand" loading="lazy"></p>
<ol>
<li>Stretch your arm out fully, palm toward you, fingers together and level.</li>
<li>Put your bottom finger on the horizon.</li>
<li>Count how many finger-widths fit between the horizon and the sun.</li>
<li>Each finger is roughly <strong>15 minutes</strong> of daylight; four fingers is about an hour.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is a rough guide that changes with your location and the season. Leave plenty of extra time. After sunset, twilight gives about 20-30 more minutes of usable light in most places.</p>
<h4>Sun basics</h4>
<ul>
<li>The sun rises roughly in the <strong>east</strong> and sets roughly in the <strong>west</strong>.</li>
<li>North of the tropics, the midday sun is in the <strong>south</strong>. South of the tropics, it is in the <strong>north</strong>.</li>
<li>Sunrise and sunset are only exactly east and west near the equinoxes (March and September). In summer they shift toward the pole; in winter, toward the equator.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Clues that are often right (Northern Hemisphere)</h4>
<p>Reverse north and south in the Southern Hemisphere.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Snow</strong> melts first on south-facing slopes and lasts longer on north-facing ones.</li>
<li>South-facing slopes are often drier, with more open, sun-loving plants. North-facing slopes are often damper with denser growth.</li>
<li><strong>Prevailing winds</strong> shape trees and snowdrifts. If you know the usual wind direction in your area, bent trees and drift shapes point the way.</li>
<li><strong>Churches:</strong> older churches in Europe often have the altar at the east end.</li>
<li><strong>Satellite dishes</strong> in North America and Europe tend to face southward.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Myths - do not rely on them</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>"Moss grows on the north side of trees."</strong> Moss grows wherever it is damp and shady, which can be any side.</li>
<li><strong>"Ant hills are on the south side."</strong> Not reliable.</li>
<li><strong>Tree rings wider on one side</strong> depend on slope, light and wind, not direction.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Combine several clues and confirm with the <a class="xl" data-id="Shadow-Stick Method">Shadow-Stick Method</a> or the stars before committing to a direction.</blockquote><h4>Northern Hemisphere: the North Star</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/north_star.jpg" alt="Finding the North Star" loading="lazy"></p>
<ol>
<li>Find the <strong>Big Dipper</strong>, a ladle shape of seven bright stars.</li>
<li>The two stars on the outer edge of the bowl are the <strong>pointers</strong>.</li>
<li>Follow the line from the bottom pointer through the top pointer, about <strong>5 times</strong> the gap between them.</li>
<li>You reach <strong>Polaris</strong>, a moderately bright star on its own. It is the end of the handle of the Little Dipper.</li>
<li>Polaris is almost exactly over the North Pole. Facing it, you face <strong>north</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the Big Dipper is low or hidden, look for <strong>Cassiopeia</strong>, a W shape on the opposite side of Polaris.</p>
<h4>Southern Hemisphere: the Southern Cross</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/southern_cross.jpg" alt="Southern Cross" loading="lazy"></p>
<ol>
<li>Find the <strong>Southern Cross</strong>, four bright stars in a kite shape. Two bright <strong>pointer stars</strong> sit beside it.</li>
<li>Extend the long axis of the cross about <strong>4.5 times</strong> its length from the foot.</li>
<li>That point is roughly the <strong>south pole of the sky</strong>. Drop straight down to the horizon: that is <strong>south</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Orion (both hemispheres)</h4>
<p>Orion's Belt, three bright stars in a row, rises almost exactly due <strong>east</strong> and sets almost due <strong>west</strong>.</p>
<h4>Any star</h4>
<p>Watch a star over two fixed sticks for 15 minutes. In the Northern Hemisphere: rising means you face east, sinking means west, moving right means south, moving left means north. Reverse north and south in the Southern Hemisphere.</p><h4>NOAA Weather Radio (US)</h4>
<ul>
<li>A network of stations broadcasting weather, hazard and civil alerts, 24 hours a day.</li>
<li>Seven channels between <strong>162.400 and 162.550 MHz</strong>. Your radio finds the strongest. Many hand-crank radios include a weather band.</li>
<li>Choose a radio with <strong>SAME</strong> (Specific Area Message Encoding). You enter your county code and it only sounds for your area.</li>
<li>Set it to alert mode so it wakes you for warnings, even overnight.</li>
<li>Keep spare batteries or a hand crank. It works when phone and internet do not.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Other ways to hear alerts</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wireless Emergency Alerts (WEA)</strong> on phones: short, loud messages for tornadoes, floods, evacuation orders and missing children. Keep them turned on.</li>
<li><strong>AM/FM radio:</strong> local stations carry Emergency Alert System messages. A battery or hand-crank radio is the dependable way to hear them. In a long disaster, many areas have one station that stays on air.</li>
<li><strong>TV and weather apps:</strong> useful if you still have power and data.</li>
<li><strong>Shortwave</strong> or <strong>foreign broadcasts</strong> may carry news if local stations are down.</li>
</ul>
<h4>What the alert words mean</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Watch:</strong> conditions are right for it to happen. Be ready.</li>
<li><strong>Warning:</strong> it is happening or about to. <strong>Act now.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Advisory:</strong> be aware and take care.</li>
</ul>
<h4>What to do when an alert sounds</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not ignore it just because the sky looks clear.</li>
<li>Follow the exact instructions: shelter, evacuate or stay inside.</li>
<li>See the guides for the hazard: <a class="xl" data-id="Tornado Safety">Tornado Safety</a>, <a class="xl" data-id="Floods and Flash Floods">Floods and Flash Floods</a>, <a class="xl" data-id="Hurricanes and Storm Surge">Hurricanes and Storm Surge</a>, <a class="xl" data-id="Wildfire and Smoke">Wildfire and Smoke</a>.</li>
<li>Tell your neighbors, especially older people and those with no phone.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Test your radio monthly. Write the frequency list in your kit.</li>
<li>Buy a model with a hand crank, solar panel and USB output, so it can charge your phone a little.</li>
</ul><blockquote>The three steps are the same for every nuclear or radiation emergency: <strong>Get inside. Stay inside. Stay tuned.</strong> Do not try to outrun fallout.</blockquote>
<h4>If you see a flash</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not look at it. Turn away and close your eyes.</li>
<li>Take cover right away behind anything solid, or drop flat and cover your head. The blast wave arrives seconds later.</li>
<li>Stay down for at least 30 seconds, then get inside.</li>
</ul>
<h4>1. Get inside</h4>
<ul>
<li>Go into the nearest solid building. Brick or concrete is best.</li>
<li>The best spots are the middle of a basement or the middle of a large building, away from outside walls and the roof.</li>
<li>More mass between you and the outdoors is better. Fallout is dust, and dust loses strength with distance and time.</li>
<li>If you are in a car, get into a building. A car does not stop radiation.</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Stay inside</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stay for at least 24 hours, unless officials tell you otherwise.</li>
<li>Close and lock the doors and windows. Turn off fans, air conditioning and heating that pull in outside air. Close the fireplace damper.</li>
<li>Move to the lowest level, in the middle of the building.</li>
<li>Fallout is strongest in the first hours and drops quickly.</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Stay tuned</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use a battery or hand-crank radio, a phone, or this device's Wi-Fi if someone has loaded updates.</li>
<li>Listen for where to go and how long to stay. Officials may tell you to move to a better shelter.</li>
<li>Do not use the phone for calls unless it is an emergency, so the network stays open.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you were outside</h4>
<ul>
<li>Take off your outer clothes before you go in. This removes most of the dust. See "Fallout: Cleaning Up and Staying Safe".</li>
<li>Wash your skin and hair with soap and water.</li>
</ul><p>Older adults, people with disabilities and people with medical conditions face more risk in a disaster and often need help earlier. Plan with them.</p>
<h4>Make a medical sheet</h4>
<p>Keep a <strong>one-page summary</strong> in a waterproof bag and in the go-bag:</p>
<ul>
<li>Name, age, photo, and emergency contacts</li>
<li>Conditions, allergies and implants</li>
<li><strong>All medicines</strong>, with doses and the pharmacy phone number</li>
<li>Doctor names and numbers</li>
<li>Insurance and ID details</li>
<li>Communication needs (hearing, vision, language) and any service animal</li>
</ul>
<h4>Medicines and supplies</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep <strong>at least 7 days</strong> of medicine, and 2 weeks if you can. Rotate it so it does not expire.</li>
<li>Keep <strong>insulin cold:</strong> a cooler with ice packs or a special pouch.</li>
<li>Spare glasses, hearing aid batteries, a cane or walker, and dentures.</li>
<li>Incontinence supplies, wipes and bags.</li>
<li>A list of supply sources and spare chargers for devices.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Power-dependent equipment</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Oxygen concentrators, ventilators and powered beds</strong> need backup power. Talk to your supplier and the power company, and register as a medical-need customer.</li>
<li>Keep a <strong>backup tank</strong> of oxygen and know how many hours it lasts. No flames or smoking near oxygen.</li>
<li>Know the nearest hospital or shelter with power, and plan how to get there before an outage.</li>
<li>See <a class="xl" data-id="Generator Safety">Generator Safety</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Carbon Monoxide">Carbon Monoxide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Getting out</h4>
<ul>
<li>Plan evacuation routes and <strong>who will help</strong>. Do not leave this to chance.</li>
<li>Ask neighbors, family or a local agency to check on you early. Share your plan and keys.</li>
<li>If someone cannot use stairs, identify a ground-floor safe room or wheelchair-friendly exit.</li>
<li>Practice moving a wheelchair up and down stairs with helpers. Do not try to carry someone without training unless it is life-threatening.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Communication</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep a pen and paper, a communication board or a phone with text. Tell responders if you are deaf, blind or non-verbal.</li>
<li>For people with dementia or autism, keep <strong>routines, familiar items and calm</strong> as much as possible, and carry ID. See <a class="xl" data-id="Helping Kids Cope">Helping Kids Cope</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Lost or Missing Child">Lost or Missing Child</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Check on neighbors</h4>
<p>Call or visit older neighbors and people who live alone <strong>before and after</strong> a disaster. Many deaths happen in the days after, in heat, cold and outages. See <a class="xl" data-id="Extreme Heat Waves">Extreme Heat Waves</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Blizzards and Ice Storms">Blizzards and Ice Storms</a>.</p><h4>First</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get the person and yourself away from the source.</strong> Open windows and get fresh air for fumes.</li>
<li>Check if they respond and are breathing. Start CPR if they are not.</li>
<li><strong>Call poison control or emergency services.</strong> In the US, Poison Control is 1-800-222-1222. Have ready: who, how old, how much, what and when.</li>
<li>Keep the container, pill bottle or plant sample to show the responders.</li>
</ol>
<h4>What to do</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Swallowed:</strong> if they are awake and alert, rinse the mouth. Give sips of water only if a poison expert says so.</li>
<li><strong>On skin:</strong> remove contaminated clothing, rinse with plenty of running water for 15-20 minutes.</li>
<li><strong>In eyes:</strong> hold the eyelid open and rinse with clean water for at least 15 minutes.</li>
<li><strong>Fumes or gas:</strong> move them into fresh air. See also the carbon monoxide guide.</li>
</ul>
<h4>What not to do</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not make them vomit.</strong> It can burn the throat a second time or cause choking.</li>
<li>Do not give salt water, milk, or home remedies.</li>
<li>Do not wait for symptoms to appear.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Opioid overdose</h4>
<ul>
<li>Signs: very slow or stopped breathing, blue lips, pinpoint pupils, cannot wake them.</li>
<li>Give <strong>naloxone (Narcan)</strong> if you have it, call emergency services and give rescue breaths or CPR. A second dose after 2-3 minutes is safe if there is no response.</li>
<li>Place them on their side if breathing. Stay until help arrives.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Recovery position</h4>
<ul>
<li>If they are unresponsive but breathing, roll them onto their side and watch their breathing.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Children are poisoned most often by medicine, cleaners and button batteries. Keep them locked away. A swallowed button battery or magnet is an emergency even with no symptoms.</blockquote><blockquote>Call a vet or a pet poison line immediately. Do not wait for symptoms. Do not induce vomiting unless a vet tells you to.</blockquote>
<h4>Dangerous to dogs</h4>
<ul>
<li>Xylitol (sugar-free gum, candy, some peanut butters). Very dangerous, even in small amounts.</li>
<li>Grapes, raisins, and currants.</li>
<li>Chocolate, especially dark chocolate and baking cocoa.</li>
<li>Onions, garlic, chives, leeks.</li>
<li>Macadamia nuts, alcohol, raw bread dough, caffeine.</li>
<li>Antifreeze (sweet-tasting) and rat poison.</li>
<li>Human medicines, especially ibuprofen, naproxen, and acetaminophen.</li>
</ul>
<h4>What to tell them</h4>
<ul>
<li>Your dog's weight, what was eaten, how much, and when.</li>
<li>Keep the package or label with you.</li>
</ul>
<p>Poison lines (a fee may apply): ASPCA Animal Poison Control 888-426-4435. Pet Poison Helpline 855-764-7661.</p><h4>Mobile phones</h4>
<ul>
<li>Try calling your emergency number (911, 112) even with no bars. Emergency calls can use any carrier's tower.</li>
<li><strong>Text messages</strong> need less signal than calls. Many regions accept texts to 911 or 112.</li>
<li>Climb high or move to open ground for a better signal.</li>
<li>Save battery: airplane mode, low brightness, and check for signal at set times.</li>
<li>Some newer phones can send emergency messages by satellite. Check your phone's emergency settings before a trip.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Common emergency radio channels (North America)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>CB:</strong> channel 9 (emergency), channel 19 (truckers)</li>
<li><strong>FRS/GMRS:</strong> channel 1 or 3 are commonly monitored in some areas. No license needed for FRS.</li>
<li><strong>Amateur (ham) 2 m:</strong> 146.520 MHz (national calling frequency). A license is normally required to transmit, but anyone may transmit in a genuine emergency.</li>
<li><strong>Marine VHF:</strong> channel 16 (156.8 MHz), distress and calling</li>
<li><strong>NOAA Weather Radio:</strong> 162.400-162.550 MHz (receive only)</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Channel use differs by country. In Europe, PMR446 radios are common, and 112 works on most phones.</blockquote>
<h4>Making a distress call</h4>
<p>Say <strong>"Mayday, Mayday, Mayday"</strong> (life-threatening) or <strong>"Pan-pan"</strong> (urgent), then: who you are, where you are, what happened, how many people, and what help you need. Repeat, then listen.</p>
<h4>Personal locator beacons (PLB)</h4>
<p>A PLB or satellite messenger sends your position to rescuers by satellite, anywhere in the world with a view of the sky. It is the most reliable way to call for help in remote areas.</p><h4>During a seizure</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stay calm and time it.</strong></li>
<li>Move hard or sharp objects away. Put something soft under their head.</li>
<li>Loosen anything tight around the neck.</li>
<li><strong>Do not hold them down</strong> and <strong>do not put anything in their mouth</strong>. They cannot swallow their tongue.</li>
<li>Stay with them until it ends.</li>
</ol>
<h4>After it stops</h4>
<ul>
<li>Roll them onto their <strong>side</strong> in the recovery position to keep the airway clear.</li>
<li>Check their breathing.</li>
<li>They will likely be confused and very tired. Speak calmly, tell them what happened, and let them rest.</li>
<li>Do not give food or drink until they are fully awake.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Get emergency help if</h4>
<ul>
<li>The seizure lasts <strong>more than 5 minutes</strong></li>
<li>Another seizure starts soon after the first</li>
<li>They do not wake up, or have trouble breathing afterward</li>
<li>It is their first seizure, or you do not know their history</li>
<li>It happened in water</li>
<li>They are injured, pregnant, or have diabetes</li>
<li>They have a high fever or recent head injury</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Seizures can also be caused by heat stroke, very low blood sugar, head injury or poisoning. Treat the cause if you can: cool someone overheated, and give sugar to a diabetic once they are awake and can swallow.</blockquote><p>Anaphylaxis is a fast, life-threatening allergic reaction, usually to stings, foods, medicines or latex. It can kill within minutes.</p>
<h4>Signs (often within minutes of contact)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Trouble breathing, wheezing, noisy breathing, tight throat, hoarse voice</li>
<li>Swelling of the lips, tongue or face</li>
<li>Hives or widespread rash, flushing</li>
<li>Dizziness, fainting, pale and floppy (especially in children)</li>
<li>Vomiting, stomach cramps</li>
<li>A feeling that something terrible is happening</li>
</ul>
<h4>What to do</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use an epinephrine auto-injector right away</strong> if the person has one. Do not wait to see if it gets worse.</li>
<li>Inject into the <strong>outer middle of the thigh</strong>. It can go through clothing. Follow the instructions printed on the device and hold it in place for the time it says.</li>
<li><strong>Call for emergency help</strong>, even if they feel better. Symptoms can come back hours later.</li>
<li><strong>Positioning:</strong></li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Lie them down with legs raised.</li>
<li>If breathing is hard, let them sit up instead.</li>
<li>If vomiting or unconscious, put them on their side.</li>
<li>Do not let them stand or walk suddenly, even if they feel better. This can cause collapse.</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>If there is no improvement after <strong>5 minutes</strong> and a second auto-injector is available, give the second one.</li>
<li>If they stop breathing normally, start <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a>.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Antihistamine tablets and inhalers do not stop anaphylaxis. Epinephrine is the treatment. Give it first; other medicines can come after.</blockquote>
<h4>Remove the cause</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stings: scrape the stinger out sideways with a fingernail or card. Do it quickly.</li>
<li>Food: do not make them vomit.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prevention</h4>
<p>Anyone with a known severe allergy should carry <strong>two</strong> auto-injectors at all times, and the people with them should know where they are and how to use them.</p><p>Heavy bleeding can kill in minutes. Act fast.</p>
<h4>1. Direct pressure</h4>
<ul>
<li>Put a cloth or gauze on the wound (bare hands if nothing else) and <strong>press hard</strong> with both hands.</li>
<li>Hold pressure for at least <strong>10 minutes</strong> without lifting to check.</li>
<li>If blood soaks through, add more material on top and keep pressing.</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Pack deep wounds</h4>
<p>For a deep wound in the thigh, armpit, groin or neck, push gauze or clean cloth <strong>into</strong> the wound, filling it, then press hard on top for at least 3 minutes (10 if plain cloth).</p>
<h4>3. Tourniquet for arms and legs</h4>
<p>Use a tourniquet when bleeding from an arm or leg is life-threatening and pressure does not stop it, or you cannot hold pressure (for example, several injured people).</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/tourniquet.jpg" alt="Tourniquet placement" loading="lazy"></p>
<ol>
<li>Place it <strong>5-7 cm (2-3 in) above the wound</strong>, never on a joint. If unsure, place it high on the limb.</li>
<li>Tighten until the bleeding <strong>stops</strong>. It will hurt a lot. That is expected.</li>
<li><strong>Write the time</strong> on the tourniquet or the person's skin.</li>
<li>Do not loosen it. Leave it for medical staff.</li>
<li>If bleeding continues, add a second tourniquet just above the first.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Improvised tourniquet</h4>
<p>A strip of cloth at least 4 cm (1.5 in) wide, tied around the limb, with a stick through the knot. Twist the stick until bleeding stops, then tie the stick in place. Thin cord or wire cuts the skin and does not work well.</p>
<blockquote>After bleeding is controlled, keep the person warm and lying down. Watch for <a class="xl" data-id="Shock">Shock</a>.</blockquote><p>This works anywhere in the world on a sunny day.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/shadow_stick.jpg" alt="Shadow-stick method" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Steps</h4>
<ol>
<li>Push a straight stick, about 1 m (3 ft) long, upright into flat ground.</li>
<li>Mark the tip of its shadow with a small stone. This is your <strong>west</strong> mark.</li>
<li>Wait <strong>15 minutes</strong> or more. The shadow tip moves.</li>
<li>Mark the new shadow tip. This is your <strong>east</strong> mark.</li>
<li>Draw a line from the first mark to the second: that line runs <strong>west to east</strong>.</li>
<li>Stand with the first mark on your left and the second on your right. You are now facing <strong>north</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>The longer you wait between marks, the more accurate the result.</li>
<li>It is most accurate around midday and near the equinoxes, and less accurate early and late in the day.</li>
<li>At local noon, the shadow is at its shortest and points roughly north (in the Northern Hemisphere) or south (in the Southern Hemisphere), away from the sun.</li>
</ul>
<p>See also: <a class="xl" data-id="Finding Direction with a Watch">Finding South with a Watch</a></p><p>In cold, wet or windy weather, shelter is your top priority after first aid. A good shelter is small, dry, out of the wind and insulated from the ground.</p>
<h4>How you lose heat</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Conduction:</strong> touching cold ground or rock. The ground steals heat fastest, so insulate under you first.</li>
<li><strong>Convection:</strong> wind blowing heat away.</li>
<li><strong>Evaporation:</strong> wet clothes and sweat.</li>
<li><strong>Radiation:</strong> heat leaving your body to the open sky.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Choosing a site</h4>
<ul>
<li>Flat, slightly raised and well-drained ground.</li>
<li>Near building materials, firewood and water, but at least 60 m (200 ft) from streams.</li>
<li>Out of the wind: behind rocks, thick trees or a ridge.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Avoid</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dead trees and hanging branches</strong> ("widowmakers") above you.</li>
<li>Dry stream beds and valley floors (floods, and cold air sinks there at night).</li>
<li>Bases of cliffs (rockfall) and avalanche slopes.</li>
<li>Insect nests and animal trails.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Pile at least 10-20 cm (4-8 in) of dry leaves, grass, pine boughs or a pack between you and the ground, even inside a tent. It compresses when you lie on it.</blockquote>
<h4>Before dark</h4>
<p>Start your shelter at least two hours before sunset. Building in the dark is slow and dangerous.</p><p>Shock happens when the body cannot get enough blood to the organs, often after bleeding, burns, severe pain or dehydration. It can kill even after the injury seems handled.</p>
<h4>Signs</h4>
<ul>
<li>Pale, cool, clammy skin</li>
<li>Fast, weak pulse and fast breathing</li>
<li>Anxiety, restlessness or confusion</li>
<li>Thirst, nausea</li>
<li>Later: drowsiness and unresponsiveness</li>
</ul>
<h4>What to do</h4>
<ol>
<li>Stop any bleeding.</li>
<li>Lay the person down on their back.</li>
<li>If there is no injury to the head, neck, back, hips or legs, you may raise the legs about 30 cm (12 in).</li>
<li><strong>Keep them warm.</strong> Put insulation under them and cover them. Cold makes shock worse.</li>
<li>Keep them calm and reassure them.</li>
<li>Do not give food or drink if they may need surgery soon. In a remote setting where help is many hours away and the person is fully alert, small sips of water are reasonable.</li>
<li>Check breathing and pulse often. Start <a class="xl" data-id="CPR">CPR</a> if they stop breathing normally.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>If vomiting or not fully awake, turn them onto their side to protect the airway.</blockquote><p>A mirror flash can be seen up to 15 km (10 miles) or more on a sunny day. It is one of the best rescue signals.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/signal_mirror.jpg" alt="Aiming a signal mirror" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Aiming without a sighting hole</h4>
<ol>
<li>Hold the mirror just under your eye, shiny side out.</li>
<li>Stretch out your other arm and make a <strong>V</strong> with two fingers. Frame the target (aircraft, boat, hilltop) inside the V.</li>
<li>Tilt the mirror until the spot of sunlight lands on your fingers.</li>
<li>Keep the light in the V and rock the mirror slightly so it sweeps across the target.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Mirrors with a sighting hole</h4>
<p>Look through the hole at the target. You will see a bright aiming spot; tilt the mirror until the spot sits on the target.</p>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Anything shiny works: a phone screen, CD, polished can lid, foil.</li>
<li>Sweep the horizon often, even when you see nothing. Pilots may see you before you see them.</li>
<li>Do not keep flashing a pilot who has clearly seen you; it can blind them. Switch to ground signals.</li>
</ul><p>At night, even a small light can be seen from far away, especially by aircraft. Night-vision equipment used by many rescue crews makes lights even easier to spot.</p>
<h4>Flashlight or headlamp</h4>
<ul>
<li>Flash <strong>SOS</strong>: three short, three long, three short. See <a class="xl" data-id="SOS, Whistle, Fire and Smoke">SOS, Whistle, Fire and Smoke</a>.</li>
<li>Point it at the aircraft or toward searchers' lights. Sweep slowly across them.</li>
<li>Save batteries: do not leave it on constantly. Signal when you hear or see searchers, and on a schedule (for example, for a minute at the top of every hour).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Glow stick on a cord</h4>
<p>Tie a glow stick to 1 m (3 ft) of cord and swing it in a big circle. It makes a large, moving ring of light that stands out from the ground.</p>
<h4>Phone</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use the flashlight function to signal.</li>
<li>A bright white screen is visible surprisingly far in the dark.</li>
<li>Turn the phone off between signals to save battery.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Fire</h4>
<p>Bright, tall flames show best at night. Keep a signal fire built and ready to light, with fast-burning fuel on top. See <a class="xl" data-id="SOS, Whistle, Fire and Smoke">SOS, Whistle, Fire and Smoke</a>.</p>
<h4>Reflectors</h4>
<p>Hang anything reflective (space blankets, reflective strips on packs and jackets) around camp. A searcher's flashlight or spotlight will make it shine.</p>
<h4>Sound travels further at night</h4>
<p>Air is often still and quiet at night. Blow your whistle in sets of three and then listen carefully for a reply.</p><p>A cold night is miserable and dangerous, and it wrecks your judgment the next day. These tricks work with or without good gear.</p>
<h4>Before bed</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Eat</strong> something, especially fats and sugars. Your body burns food to make heat. Keep a snack by your bed for the middle of the night.</li>
<li><strong>Drink</strong> warm water. Being dehydrated makes you colder.</li>
<li><strong>Pee</strong> before you lie down. Your body wastes heat keeping a full bladder warm.</li>
<li><strong>Warm up</strong> with light exercise (not to the point of sweating) right before getting in.</li>
<li><strong>Change into dry clothes</strong>, especially socks. Put damp clothes in your bag to dry them with body heat if you have no other way.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Under you matters most</h4>
<p>The ground pulls heat from you faster than the air. Put down <strong>at least 10-20 cm (4-8 in)</strong> of dry insulation: leaves, grass, pine boughs, a foam pad, an empty pack, a coiled rope. Then more.</p>
<h4>Around you</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wear a <strong>hat</strong> and cover your neck.</li>
<li>Fill air gaps in your sleeping bag or blankets with spare clothes.</li>
<li>Fill a bottle with hot water (check the lid seals and the bottle can take heat), wrap it in a sock, and put it between your thighs or on your stomach.</li>
<li>Keep your face out of the bag; breath adds moisture that makes it colder.</li>
<li>Huddle together with others if you can.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Through the night</h4>
<ul>
<li>If you wake up cold, eat, do some sit-ups or clench and relax your muscles, then try again.</li>
<li>Keep tomorrow's water and boots inside your shelter or bag so they do not freeze.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Shivering is a warning. If someone shivers hard and cannot stop, or starts stumbling and mumbling, treat for <a class="xl" data-id="Hypothermia and Frostbite">Hypothermia</a> now.</blockquote><p>Most snakebites are not fatal, especially with calm, correct first aid and medical care.</p>
<h4>Do</h4>
<ol>
<li>Move away from the snake. Do not try to catch or kill it; a photo from a distance is enough.</li>
<li>Keep the person calm and as still as possible.</li>
<li>Remove rings, watches and tight clothing from the bitten limb before swelling starts.</li>
<li>Keep the bitten limb still and at about heart level. Splint it if you can.</li>
<li>Mark the edge of swelling with a pen every 30 minutes, with the time.</li>
<li>Get to medical care as fast as safely possible. Carrying the person is better than letting them walk, if practical.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Do not</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cut the wound or try to suck out venom</li>
<li>Use a tourniquet</li>
<li>Apply ice or heat</li>
<li>Give alcohol or medicines like aspirin</li>
<li>Use electric shock or "snakebite kits"</li>
</ul>
<h4>Regional note</h4>
<p>In <strong>Australia</strong>, and for some snakes elsewhere (such as coral snakes, cobras and mambas), a <strong>pressure immobilization bandage</strong> is advised: wrap a wide elastic bandage firmly from the bite up the whole limb, then splint. Do <strong>not</strong> use this for North American rattlesnakes and other pit vipers, where it can increase tissue damage.</p>
<blockquote>Signs needing urgent help: trouble breathing or swallowing, drooping eyelids, bleeding gums, vomiting, fainting, rapidly spreading swelling.</blockquote><h4>Why snow</h4>
<p>Snow is a good insulator. Inside a snow shelter it can be around 0 C (32 F) while the wind chill outside is far below. Pick a spot out of the wind, away from avalanche slopes.</p>
<h4>Snow cave (needs a deep drift)</h4>
<ol>
<li>Find a drift <strong>at least 2 m (6-7 ft) deep</strong>, packed firm.</li>
<li>Dig a tunnel in, then upward to a sleeping platform higher than the entrance. Cold air sinks and warm air stays.</li>
<li>Make the roof a smooth dome, <strong>at least 30 cm (1 ft) thick</strong>, so it doesn't drip or collapse.</li>
<li>Poke a <strong>ventilation hole</strong> the size of a fist through the roof. Keep it open.</li>
<li>Block most of the doorway with a pack or blocks of snow. Keep a shovel inside.</li>
<li>Mark the outside with a pole or skis so rescuers can find it.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Quinzee (when there is no drift)</h4>
<ol>
<li>Pile up snow about 2 m (6 ft) high and 3 m (10 ft) wide. Let it settle for an hour or two.</li>
<li>Push sticks about 30 cm (1 ft) long into the pile as thickness guides.</li>
<li>Hollow it out from a doorway, stopping when you reach the sticks. Smooth the ceiling.</li>
<li>Add a vent hole and block the door loosely.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Staying safe</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep the vent clear.</strong> Check it often. Wet snow, a snowfall or a candle's use can block it. Poor air kills.</li>
<li>Do not use a stove or burn fuel inside without good ventilation. Fumes and carbon monoxide.</li>
<li>Dig carefully and stay dry. Take off outer layers while you work so you don't sweat. Put them back on before you rest.</li>
<li>Put insulation under you: branches, packs, ropes, a pad.</li>
<li>Keep your tools inside so you can dig out.</li>
<li>A single candle can warm a small cave, but keep the vent open.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Building takes 1-2 hours of hard work. Start before dark and before you are exhausted.</blockquote><p>A solar still uses the sun to draw moisture from soil and plants. It makes small amounts, but the water is distilled and clean.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/solar_still.jpg" alt="Cross-section of a solar still" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>Building it</h4>
<ol>
<li>Dig a bowl-shaped hole about 90 cm (3 ft) wide and 60 cm (2 ft) deep, in a sunny spot with damp soil if possible.</li>
<li>Put a container in the center at the bottom.</li>
<li>Add green, non-poisonous plants around the container (not in it) to add moisture.</li>
<li>Cover the hole with clear plastic. Seal the edges with soil and rocks so no air escapes.</li>
<li>Put a small rock in the middle of the plastic so it forms a cone right above the container.</li>
<li>Water condenses on the plastic, runs down to the low point and drips into the container.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Use a drinking tube from the container to the outside so you do not need to open the still.</li>
<li>You can pour salt water or urine into the pit (not the container). The still separates clean water.</li>
<li>Build several stills if you rely on them.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>A still often yields well under a liter per day, and digging makes you sweat. In hot deserts, do the work at dawn or dusk, and only if there is no better source.</blockquote><h4>The rule of three</h4>
<p><strong>Three of anything</strong> means distress: three fires in a triangle, three whistle blasts, three gunshots, three flashes.</p>
<h4>SOS in Morse code</h4>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/sos_morse.jpg" alt="SOS in Morse code" loading="lazy"></p>
<p>Three short, three long, three short: `... --- ...`. Pause, then repeat. Use a flashlight, mirror, whistle or car horn.</p>
<h4>Whistle</h4>
<p>A whistle carries much farther than shouting and uses less energy. Blow <strong>three long blasts</strong>, then pause and listen. Repeat regularly. The answer is often two blasts.</p>
<h4>Signal fires</h4>
<ul>
<li>Build three fires in a triangle, about 30 m (100 ft) apart, in an open area.</li>
<li>Keep them ready to light: tinder and kindling under cover, with plenty of fuel nearby.</li>
<li><strong>Day:</strong> add green leaves, moss or wet wood to make thick <strong>white smoke</strong>, which shows against dark forest. Rubber or oil makes <strong>black smoke</strong>, which shows against snow or sand.</li>
<li><strong>Night:</strong> bright flames show best.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Only light a signal fire when you hear or see a search aircraft or party, unless you have endless fuel. Never risk starting a wildfire.</blockquote>
<h4>Other signals</h4>
<ul>
<li>Bright clothing or a space blanket spread out in the open.</li>
<li>Flashlight or headlamp at night, in SOS pattern.</li>
<li>Move: motion is easier to spot than a still person.</li>
</ul><h4>Suspect a spinal injury if</h4>
<ul>
<li>A fall from height, a diving or sports injury, a car crash, or a hard blow to the head or back</li>
<li>Neck or back pain, tenderness or a deformity</li>
<li>Numbness, tingling or weakness in arms or legs, or they cannot move them</li>
<li>Loss of control of bladder or bowel</li>
<li>They are unconscious and you do not know what happened</li>
</ul>
<h4>What to do</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tell them not to move.</strong> Call emergency services.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the head, neck and back in line.</strong> Kneel behind their head and hold it gently still with both hands, or place rolled towels or clothing on both sides of the head.</li>
<li>Do not bend, twist or lift. Do not remove a helmet unless it blocks breathing.</li>
<li>Check breathing. Keep them warm with a blanket.</li>
<li>Stay with them and talk calmly until help arrives.</li>
</ol>
<h4>If they are not breathing</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Airway first.</strong> Open it by lifting the jaw forward without tilting the head. If that does not work, tilt the head back just enough to open the airway. Breathing matters more than the spine.</li>
<li>Start CPR.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you must move them</h4>
<p>Only move them for immediate danger such as fire, rising water or a collapsing structure, or to place an unconscious person on their side so they do not choke.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use at least 3 people. Roll them as one piece, keeping head, neck and back in a straight line (<strong>log roll</strong>).</li>
<li>If you are alone, drag them by the shoulders or clothing along their length, never sideways.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>If someone is alone and far from help, think carefully. A person who can feel and move all four limbs and has no neck pain is less likely to have a serious injury, but when in doubt, keep them still.</blockquote><h4>Find dry fuel</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dead branches still on the tree</strong> stay drier than wood on the ground. Snap them: a crisp snap means dry inside.</li>
<li>Look under overhangs, dense conifers, logs or fallen trees.</li>
<li>Split wood open. The <strong>inside is dry</strong> even when the outside is wet.</li>
<li><strong>Fatwood</strong> (resin-rich pine stump or branch base) and <strong>birch bark</strong> burn even when wet. Look for dead standing wood and dry bark.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Make dry tinder</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Feather sticks:</strong> shave thin curls on a dry stick, leaving them attached. Fuzzy curls catch easily.</li>
<li>Pocket lint, cotton balls with petroleum jelly, dryer lint, and fire starters work well.</li>
<li>Dry grass, bark and leaves from a protected spot, or inside your clothing.</li>
<li>Keep tinder in a bag or in your jacket until you need it.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Build the fire</h4>
<ol>
<li>Choose a spot out of the rain: under a tree with a canopy, a rock overhang or beneath a tarp.</li>
<li><strong>Make a platform</strong> of flat stones, green logs or bark so the fire isn't on wet ground.</li>
<li>Use a small amount of tinder and have lots of kindling, from pencil-thin to thumb-thick, ready before you strike.</li>
<li>Build a <strong>teepee</strong> or lean-to against a log. Start thin and build up slowly.</li>
<li>Light it, shelter it with your body or a windbreak, and add pencil-thin sticks as it catches.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Keep it going</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stack wet wood next to the fire to dry before you add it.</li>
<li>Feed small sticks. Don't smother it with big pieces.</li>
<li>Reflect heat with a wall of logs or stones behind the fire.</li>
<li>Keep spare dry wood under cover for later.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Rain extinguishes a weak fire fast. Do not start until you have enough fuel ready, from tinder to wrist-thick pieces.</blockquote><p>Survival is mostly mental. People with very little gear have survived because they kept their heads. People with plenty have died because they panicked.</p>
<h4>Calm yourself in a minute</h4>
<p><strong>Box breathing:</strong> breathe in for 4 seconds, hold for 4, breathe out for 4, hold for 4. Repeat four or five times. It slows your heart and helps you think.</p>
<p>Then use <a class="xl" data-id="STOP - Stop, Think, Observe, Plan">STOP</a>: Stop, Think, Observe, Plan.</p>
<h4>Keep going</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give yourself small goals.</strong> "Get the shelter roof done before dark" is easier than "survive."</li>
<li><strong>Keep a routine:</strong> set times to check signals, collect water and firewood, eat and sleep.</li>
<li><strong>Stay busy.</strong> Useful work keeps fear down.</li>
<li><strong>Look after your body.</strong> Cold, thirst, hunger and lack of sleep all make your thinking worse. Fix them early.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a log.</strong> Write the date, what you did, and what you plan. It helps you think, and helps rescuers later.</li>
</ul>
<h4>In a group</h4>
<ul>
<li>Choose one leader to make final decisions, and listen to everyone first.</li>
<li>Give every person a job, even children and injured people. Feeling useful matters.</li>
<li>Check on each other. Watch for anyone who goes quiet, stops eating or wanders off.</li>
<li>Keep the group together. Never let anyone leave camp alone without saying where and when they will be back.</li>
</ul>
<h4>With children</h4>
<ul>
<li>Stay calm in front of them. They take their cues from you.</li>
<li>Explain simply and honestly what is happening and what the plan is.</li>
<li>Give them simple tasks and praise.</li>
<li>Teach them: <strong>if you are lost, stay put, and hug a tree.</strong> Make noise, answer anyone calling your name, and do not hide from searchers.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Rescue usually comes. Most people who are reported missing are found within a day or two.</blockquote><p>Most survival deaths come from bad decisions made in the first hour. When things go wrong, use <strong>STOP</strong>.</p>
<h4>S - Stop</h4>
<p>Sit down. Drink some water, eat something small. Do not keep walking. Panic burns energy and makes you more lost.</p>
<h4>T - Think</h4>
<ul>
<li>What happened, and what do you actually have?</li>
<li>Does anyone know where you are and when you are due back?</li>
<li>What is the biggest danger right now?</li>
</ul>
<h4>O - Observe</h4>
<ul>
<li>Weather: is it getting colder, darker, wetter?</li>
<li>Terrain: water sources, shelter material, open areas for signaling.</li>
<li>Your body: injuries, warmth, thirst, how much daylight is left.</li>
<li>Your gear: empty your pockets and pack. Everything counts.</li>
</ul>
<h4>P - Plan</h4>
<p>Decide on one clear plan and follow it. Re-check it every few hours.</p>
<blockquote>If someone knows your route and expected return, it is usually best to <strong>stay put</strong>. Searchers find people who stay in one place far more often.</blockquote>
<h4>Stay or go?</h4>
<p>Stay if: people will look for you, you are injured, the weather is bad, or you do not know which way to go.</p>
<p>Move only if: no one will search for you, your location is unsafe, or you know a clear route to help. If you move, leave a note or arrow showing your direction.</p><h4>How much</h4>
<ul>
<li>At least <strong>4 liters (1 gallon) per person per day</strong>: about half for drinking, half for cooking and washing.</li>
<li>Store at least <strong>3 days' worth</strong>. <strong>Two weeks</strong> is better for home.</li>
<li>Add more for hot climates, children, nursing mothers, sick people and pets.</li>
</ul>
<p>Example: a family of 4 for 2 weeks needs about 56 gallons (210 liters).</p>
<h4>Containers</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best:</strong> sealed, store-bought bottled water. Keep it until the expiry date.</li>
<li>Food-grade water containers (look for "food grade" or the recycle symbol 1, 2 or 4).</li>
<li>Clean 2-liter soda bottles work well.</li>
<li><strong>Do not use</strong> milk or juice jugs (they hold bacteria and break down), or containers that held chemicals.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Filling your own containers</h4>
<ol>
<li>Wash the container with soap and hot water, and rinse.</li>
<li>Sanitize: swish a solution of 1 teaspoon of unscented bleach in 1 liter (1 quart) of water around the inside, then empty it.</li>
<li>Fill with clean tap water. If your water is not chlorinated (for example, a well), add 2 drops of plain unscented bleach per liter.</li>
<li>Cap tightly without touching the inside of the cap.</li>
<li>Label it with the date.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Keeping it good</h4>
<ul>
<li>Store in a cool, dark place, away from fuels and chemicals (plastic lets fumes through).</li>
<li>Replace home-filled water <strong>every 6 months</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Hidden water in your home</h4>
<p>In an emergency you can also use:</p>
<ul>
<li>The water heater tank (turn off the power or gas first, open the drain at the bottom)</li>
<li>Water in pipes: open the highest tap in the house, then collect from the lowest</li>
<li>Ice cubes</li>
<li>The toilet <strong>tank</strong> (not the bowl), only if no chemicals or cleaning tablets are in it, and treat it first</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Treat any of these before drinking. See <a class="xl" data-id="Making Water Safe to Drink">Making Water Safe to Drink</a>. Never drink from waterbeds, pools or radiators.</blockquote><h4>First: stay with the vehicle</h4>
<p>A car is shelter and is easier for rescuers to spot than a person. <strong>Do not walk away</strong> unless help is clearly within a short, visible distance. Whiteouts make people lose their way within meters of the road.</p>
<h4>Make it easy to find you</h4>
<ul>
<li>Turn on the hazard lights and call for help if you have a signal. Say exactly where you are.</li>
<li>Tie a bright cloth to the antenna or a window. Raise the hood when the storm ends.</li>
<li>Clear snow from the car roof to help aircraft see it. See <a class="xl" data-id="Helping Searchers Find You">Helping Searchers Find You</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Keep warm</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Run the engine about 10 minutes every hour</strong> for heat. Crack a window on the side away from the wind.</li>
<li><strong>Before each time you run the engine, clear snow from the tailpipe.</strong> A blocked tailpipe fills the car with carbon monoxide. See <a class="xl" data-id="Carbon Monoxide">Carbon Monoxide</a>.</li>
<li>Do not run the engine for long periods and do not fall into a deep sleep with it running. Take turns staying awake if you are with others.</li>
<li>Wear every layer you have. Use floor mats, maps, seat covers and newspapers for insulation. Put on a hat. Huddle together.</li>
<li>Move your arms and legs often. Stay dry.</li>
<li>Keep fluids up. Do not eat snow, which cools you.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Fuel</h4>
<p>Only run the engine as much as needed, so fuel lasts. Turn it off in between and keep the dome light and radio use low to save the battery.</p>
<h4>Watch for cold injury</h4>
<p>See <a class="xl" data-id="Hypothermia and Frostbite">Hypothermia and Frostbite</a>. Shivering that stops, confusion and drowsiness mean it is serious.</p>
<h4>Prevent it</h4>
<p>Check forecasts, keep the fuel tank over half full in winter and carry a kit. See <a class="xl" data-id="Car Emergency Kit">Car Emergency Kit</a>.</p><h4>Stroke: think FAST</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>F</strong>ace: one side droops or is numb when they smile.</li>
<li><strong>A</strong>rms: one arm drifts down or is weak when both are raised.</li>
<li><strong>S</strong>peech: slurred, strange, or they cannot understand you.</li>
<li><strong>T</strong>ime: note when it started and <strong>call emergency services now</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Other signs: sudden confusion, trouble seeing, dizziness or loss of balance, a sudden severe headache.</p>
<p><strong>Do:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Sit or lay them down with the head slightly raised. Keep them calm.</li>
<li><strong>Note the time symptoms began.</strong> Treatment depends on it.</li>
<li>Loosen tight clothing. If they are unconscious but breathing, place them on their side.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Do not:</strong> give food, drink or pills (they may not swallow safely), and do not let them walk or drive.</p>
<h4>Heart attack</h4>
<p><strong>Signs:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Pressure, squeezing or pain in the chest that may spread to the arm, jaw, neck or back</li>
<li>Shortness of breath, cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness</li>
<li>In women, older adults and diabetics, the signs can be milder: unusual tiredness, nausea, back or jaw pain</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Do:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Call emergency services.</strong> Do not wait to see if it passes.</li>
<li>Have them sit and rest in a comfortable position, often half upright.</li>
<li><strong>Aspirin:</strong> if they are not allergic and are awake, have them <strong>chew one adult aspirin (325 mg)</strong>, or 2 low-dose (81 mg) tablets. Do not give it if they have bleeding problems or have been told not to take it.</li>
<li>If prescribed nitroglycerin, help them take their own.</li>
<li><strong>If they collapse and stop breathing normally, start CPR.</strong> Use an AED if one is available.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Do not drive them yourself if an ambulance is possible. Responders can start treatment on the way.</blockquote><p>A tarp is the fastest shelter there is. Even a large trash bag or emergency blanket can become a roof.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/tarp_shelter.jpg" alt="Tarp A-frame" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>A-frame</h4>
<ol>
<li>Tie a cord tight between two trees at waist to chest height.</li>
<li>Hang the tarp over the cord so both sides are equal.</li>
<li>Stake or weight the corners down. Pull them out until the tarp is tight.</li>
<li>In wind, set the windward side lower or right to the ground.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Lean-to tarp</h4>
<p>Tie the top edge of the tarp along the ridgeline and stake the bottom edge to the ground on the windward side. Good with a fire in front.</p>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>No grommets? Wrap a small stone in the tarp corner and tie the cord around it.</li>
<li>Angle the roof steeply so rain runs off.</li>
<li>Dig a shallow trench uphill of the shelter to steer water around it.</li>
<li>Tie cord with a knot you can untie: a taut-line hitch or truckers hitch lets you tighten the line.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>An emergency blanket reflects heat back to you, but it holds condensation. Wrap it outside your insulation, not against your skin, when you can.</blockquote><p>In an emergency, deal with the biggest threat first. The Rule of 3s is a rough guide to how long a person can last without each basic need.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/rule_of_threes.jpg" alt="The Rule of 3s" loading="lazy"></p>
<h4>The order</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Air</strong> - about 3 minutes. Breathing problems, choking, drowning, smoke and severe bleeding come first.</li>
<li><strong>Shelter</strong> - about 3 hours in harsh weather. Cold, wet and wind kill faster than thirst. Stay dry and insulated.</li>
<li><strong>Water</strong> - about 3 days. Less in heat or with hard work.</li>
<li><strong>Food</strong> - about 3 weeks. Food matters for morale and energy, but it is the last priority.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>These are averages, not promises. Cold water, heat, injury, age and illness shorten every limit.</blockquote>
<h4>Using it</h4>
<ul>
<li>Before you build anything, check for immediate danger: fire, rising water, traffic, falling rocks.</li>
<li>Treat life-threatening injuries next.</li>
<li>Then protect yourself from the weather, then find water.</li>
<li>Spend energy on food only once the first three are handled.</li>
</ul>
<p>See also: <a class="xl" data-id="STOP - Stop, Think, Observe, Plan">STOP: Stop, Think, Observe, Plan</a></p><h4>Ticks</h4>
<p>Ticks can spread Lyme disease and other illnesses. The sooner you remove one, the lower the risk.</p>
<p><img src="/wiki/img/tick_removal.jpg" alt="Removing a tick" loading="lazy"></p>
<ol>
<li>Use fine-tipped tweezers. Grab the tick <strong>as close to the skin as possible</strong>.</li>
<li>Pull <strong>straight up</strong> with slow, steady pressure. Do not twist or jerk.</li>
<li>If mouth parts stay in the skin and you cannot easily remove them, leave them; the skin will heal.</li>
<li>Clean the bite and your hands with soap and water or alcohol.</li>
<li>Do not use heat, nail polish or petroleum jelly. They make the tick release more fluid into you.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Watch for a spreading rash (often a "bull's-eye" ring), fever, chills, aches or tiredness in the next 30 days. Get medical care if they appear and say you were bitten.</blockquote>
<p><strong>Prevention:</strong> long sleeves and trousers tucked into socks, repellent, and a full-body tick check every evening, including scalp, armpits, groin and behind knees.</p>
<h4>Bee and wasp stings</h4>
<ol>
<li>Scrape out a bee stinger quickly with a fingernail or card edge.</li>
<li>Wash the area.</li>
<li>Cool with a cold, wet cloth to ease swelling and pain.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for anaphylaxis:</strong> trouble breathing, face or throat swelling, dizziness, hives spreading. See <a class="xl" data-id="Severe Allergic Reaction (Anaphylaxis)">Severe Allergic Reaction</a>.</li>
<li>Many stings at once, or a sting inside the mouth or throat, needs urgent help.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Spider bites</h4>
<p>Most spider bites are harmless. Clean, cool and watch them.</p>
<p>Get medical care for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Widow spiders</strong> (shiny black, red hourglass mark): severe muscle cramps, stomach pain, sweating.</li>
<li><strong>Recluse spiders</strong> (brown, violin shape on the back): a bite that turns into a spreading, dark or open sore over hours or days.</li>
<li>Any bite with spreading redness, fever or feeling unwell.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Scorpions</h4>
<p>Clean the sting and cool it. Most cause sharp pain only. Get urgent help for children, or for anyone with numbness spreading, twitching, roving eye movements, drooling or trouble breathing.</p><p>A tornado can form with little warning. Know where you will go <strong>before</strong> the sky turns.</p>
<h4>Watch and warning</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Watch:</strong> conditions are right for tornadoes. Stay alert and know your shelter.</li>
<li><strong>Warning:</strong> a tornado is happening or about to. <strong>Take shelter now.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h4>Signs</h4>
<ul>
<li>Dark, greenish sky and large hail</li>
<li>A rotating, low cloud or a funnel</li>
<li>A loud, steady roar like a freight train</li>
<li>A cloud of debris near the ground, even if you see no funnel</li>
</ul>
<h4>Where to go</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best:</strong> a basement or storm cellar. Get under sturdy furniture or a workbench.</li>
<li><strong>No basement:</strong> the lowest floor, in a small interior room with no windows (bathroom, closet, hallway). Put as many walls as you can between you and the outside.</li>
<li><strong>Cover up:</strong> use a mattress, blankets or a bike helmet. Protect your head and neck with your arms.</li>
<li><strong>Mobile homes and vehicles are not safe.</strong> Get to a sturdy building before the storm arrives.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are caught outside</h4>
<ul>
<li>Do not try to outrun a tornado in a car in traffic or a city.</li>
<li>If there is no building, lie flat in the lowest ground you can find (a ditch), face down, and cover your head and neck with your hands.</li>
<li><strong>Do not shelter under a highway overpass.</strong> Wind speeds can be higher there.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Afterward</h4>
<ul>
<li>Expect more storms. Stay in shelter until the warning ends.</li>
<li>Watch for downed power lines, broken glass, nails and gas leaks. See <a class="xl" data-id="Downed Power Lines">Downed Power Lines</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Gas Leak">Gas Leak</a>.</li>
<li>Check for injuries and give first aid. See <a class="xl" data-id="First Aid Priorities">First Aid Priorities</a>.</li>
<li>Text instead of calling so lines stay open for emergencies.</li>
</ul><h4>Before you go</h4>
<p>Tell someone your route and when to expect you. Carry a map of the area.</p>
<h4>Marking your trail</h4>
<ul>
<li>Break branches at eye level, pointing your direction.</li>
<li>Stack small rock piles (cairns) or make arrows with sticks.</li>
<li>Leave notes at junctions: time, date, direction and condition.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are lost and decide to move</h4>
<ul>
<li>Pick one direction and stick to it. People who wander in circles get more lost.</li>
<li>Go downhill and follow water: streams lead to rivers, and rivers to roads and towns. But avoid steep canyons and waterfalls.</li>
<li>Climb to a high point (safely) to look for roads, buildings, smoke or power lines.</li>
<li>Follow power lines, fences, pipelines or railways. They lead somewhere.</li>
<li>Travel in daylight. Stop and make camp well before dark.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>In fog, darkness or a whiteout, stop and wait. Walking blind leads to falls and getting more lost.</blockquote>
<h4>Signs of people</h4>
<p>Roads, trails, fences, cut stumps, tire tracks, livestock, and the sound of traffic, dogs or machines.</p><p>This device can send short text messages to other Survival Wiki boards nearby, with <strong>no internet, phone signal or router</strong>.</p>
<h4>How it works</h4>
<ul>
<li>Boards talk to each other directly using a short-range radio (ESP-NOW, on the same 2.4 GHz band as Wi-Fi).</li>
<li><strong>Every board relays messages it hears.</strong> A message can hop through several boards, so more boards means more range.</li>
<li>Messages are short, up to about 64 characters.</li>
<li>The sender sees <strong>"Heard by"</strong>: which boards received the message.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Using it on the board</h4>
<ol>
<li>Tap <strong>Mesh</strong> on the home screen.</li>
<li>Make sure mesh is turned on, then pick a quick message or type your own.</li>
<li>Watch for replies. A star on the Mesh button means you have unread messages.</li>
<li>The <strong>Nearby</strong> screen shows boards that are around you and a rough distance.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Using it from your phone</h4>
<ol>
<li>Turn on the board's Wi-Fi hotspot and join it with your phone.</li>
<li>Open the chat page. Type longer messages on your phone and the board sends them over the mesh.</li>
<li>The phone also gets maps and the wiki from the board.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Range</h4>
<ul>
<li>A few hundred meters between two boards in the open is typical. Buildings, hills and trees cut that sharply.</li>
<li>Raise the board up high, and keep it away from metal and your body.</li>
<li>Space your group out, but keep every board within reach of at least one other.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Tips</h4>
<ul>
<li>Agree on a short check-in time so batteries last: for example, 10 minutes past each hour.</li>
<li>Keep messages short and clear. Say where you are and what you need.</li>
<li>It is a <strong>short-range text tool</strong>, not a replacement for a phone or a radio call for help. For wide-area help, see <a class="xl" data-id="Choosing an Emergency Radio">Choosing an Emergency Radio</a> and <a class="xl" data-id="Radio and Phone Tips">Radio and Phone Tips</a>.</li>
<li>Power matters: a USB power bank can run a board for many hours.</li>
</ul><p>Everyone in the home should know where these are and how they work. Find them <strong>today</strong>, not in an emergency.</p>
<h4>Water</h4>
<ul>
<li>The main valve is usually where the water line enters the house: a basement, a crawl space, a utility room, by the water heater, or in a box near the street.</li>
<li>A wheel handle turns <strong>clockwise</strong> to close. A lever handle closes when it is <strong>crossways</strong> to the pipe.</li>
<li>Also find the small shutoffs under sinks and behind toilets.</li>
<li>Close the main for a burst pipe, or if told your water is contaminated. See <a class="xl" data-id="Burst and Frozen Pipes">Burst and Frozen Pipes</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Electricity</h4>
<ul>
<li>The breaker panel is usually in a garage, basement, utility room or hallway. Know where the <strong>main breaker</strong> is.</li>
<li>Turn off individual breakers first, then the main last.</li>
<li><strong>If the panel is wet, or you are standing in water, do not touch it.</strong> Call the utility and an electrician.</li>
<li>After a flood or fire, do not restore power until it has been inspected.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Gas</h4>
<ul>
<li>The meter has a main valve. A quarter turn so the handle is <strong>crossways</strong> to the pipe shuts it off. You may need a wrench.</li>
<li>Shut it off only if you smell gas or are told to, and you can reach it safely. <strong>Only the gas company should turn it back on.</strong> See <a class="xl" data-id="Gas Leak">Gas Leak</a>.</li>
<li>Propane: close the tank valve clockwise.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Get ready</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep a <strong>wrench</strong> or valve key near the valve, and tie it to the pipe.</li>
<li><strong>Label</strong> each shutoff with a tag. Mark the breaker panel too.</li>
<li>Show everyone in the family, including older children. Take a photo of each and keep it in your go-bag.</li>
<li>Look at the valve once a year and turn it a bit to make sure it still moves.</li>
</ul><p>A car fire can spread in under a minute. Plastics, fuel and tires burn hot and fast. <strong>People matter more than the car.</strong></p>
<h4>If you see smoke or flames</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pull over</strong> to a safe spot as soon as you can, away from buildings and dry grass. Turn on the hazard lights.</li>
<li><strong>Turn off the engine.</strong> This stops fuel and spark.</li>
<li><strong>Get everyone out.</strong> Take children and pets. Leave your belongings.</li>
<li><strong>Move at least 30 m (100 ft) away</strong>, upwind and away from traffic. Tires, fuel tanks and shocks can burst or explode.</li>
<li><strong>Call emergency services</strong> with your exact location.</li>
<li>Keep everyone back, away from the road.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Do not</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not open the hood.</strong> The sudden rush of air can make the fire flare.</li>
<li>Do not go back for belongings.</li>
<li>Do not use a small extinguisher on anything larger than a very small fire. If you have one and the fire is tiny and not near the fuel or passenger area, you may use it from a safe distance. Otherwise, leave.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you cannot get out</h4>
<ul>
<li>If your seat belt is jammed, cut it with a seat belt cutter or knife if you have one.</li>
<li>Use a window-breaker tool on a side window (not the windshield). See <a class="xl" data-id="Car in Water">Car in Water</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If your clothing catches fire</h4>
<p><strong>Stop, drop and roll.</strong> Cover your face with your hands and roll to put it out. See <a class="xl" data-id="Burns">Burns</a>.</p>
<h4>Prevent it</h4>
<ul>
<li>Fix fuel and oil leaks and frayed wiring.</li>
<li>Carry a small fire extinguisher rated for car use in the passenger compartment, not the trunk.</li>
<li>Do not leave lighters, aerosol cans or batteries in a hot car.</li>
</ul><p>Carry at least two ways to make fire. Friction methods are a last resort, not a plan.</p>
<h4>Lighter and matches</h4>
<ul>
<li>A cheap disposable lighter is the most reliable tool. Keep one in a warm pocket; cold lighters fail.</li>
<li>Waterproof your matches in a sealed container. Strike away from your body and shield the flame with your hands.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Ferro rod (fire steel)</h4>
<ol>
<li>Place the rod tip right in the tinder.</li>
<li>Hold the striker still and pull the rod back hard, so sparks fall into the tinder without knocking it apart.</li>
<li>Use very fine, dry tinder: cotton with petroleum jelly, cattail fluff, fine bark shavings.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Battery and steel wool</h4>
<p>Touch both terminals of a 9-volt battery to fine steel wool, or bridge a flashlight battery with a strand. The wool glows and lights tinder. Keep steel wool away from batteries when packing.</p>
<h4>Lens</h4>
<p>A magnifying glass, eyeglasses for far-sightedness, or a clear bottle of water can focus sunlight onto dark tinder. Needs bright sun.</p>
<h4>Bow drill (last resort)</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Fireboard:</strong> dry, soft, dead wood (willow, cedar, poplar, basswood) about 2 cm thick.</li>
<li><strong>Spindle:</strong> straight, dry stick of the same wood, thumb-thick, about 20 cm long.</li>
<li><strong>Bearing block:</strong> a hard piece of wood or stone with a dent, to press down on the spindle.</li>
<li><strong>Bow:</strong> a curved stick about arm's length with cord tied loosely so you can twist the spindle into it.</li>
<li>Cut a notch from the edge of the fireboard to a small burned-in hole. Spin the spindle fast with long, steady bow strokes while pressing down.</li>
<li>When smoking dust fills the notch and keeps smoking on its own, you have an ember. Tip it into a tinder bundle and blow gently until it flames.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Friction fire takes practice and a lot of energy. Learn it before you need it.</blockquote><p>Wildfires can move faster than you can run and can throw embers a mile ahead of the flames.</p>
<h4>Get ready</h4>
<ul>
<li>Keep a go-bag by the door with medicines, documents, water, a flashlight, a radio and an N95 mask.</li>
<li>Know two routes out. Keep the car fueled and parked facing out.</li>
<li>Clear dry leaves, pine needles and brush from around the house and gutters.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are told to evacuate</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Go early.</strong> Do not wait to see the flames. Roads can fill or be blocked quickly.</li>
<li>Close windows and doors but leave them unlocked. Turn off gas and propane if you can do so quickly.</li>
<li>Take pets. Tell someone where you are going.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are trapped in a vehicle</h4>
<ul>
<li>Park in a clear area away from trees and brush, if you can, and not on the road.</li>
<li>Close all windows and vents. Turn off the fan and air conditioning.</li>
<li>Lie on the floor below the window line and cover yourself with a blanket or coat (wool is best).</li>
<li>Stay inside. A car is a better shield than the open air. The fire front usually passes in minutes.</li>
<li>Do not drive through heavy smoke or flames.</li>
</ul>
<h4>If you are on foot</h4>
<ul>
<li>Go to an area that has already burned, bare ground, a rock slide, or a body of water.</li>
<li>Avoid steep slopes and canyons. Fire runs uphill fast.</li>
<li>Lie face down on bare ground, cover your head and keep your mouth and nose covered with wet cloth.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Smoke</h4>
<ul>
<li>Smoke can hurt hours or days away from the fire. Stay inside with windows and doors closed.</li>
<li>Use a <strong>N95 or P100 respirator</strong> if you must be out. Cloth and dust masks do not stop smoke particles.</li>
<li>Run the air conditioner on recirculate, or a HEPA filter, if you have one.</li>
<li>Seek care for chest pain, trouble breathing or severe coughing.</li>
<li>Drink plenty of water and avoid hard exercise in smoky air.</li>
</ul><p>In the wild, infection is a bigger long-term danger than most cuts.</p>
<h4>Cleaning</h4>
<ol>
<li>Stop the bleeding with pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Irrigate</strong> with lots of drinkable water: at least 1 liter for a wound. Squirt it with force, for example from a bag with a pinhole or a syringe.</li>
<li>Remove dirt and splinters with clean tweezers.</li>
<li>Clean the skin around the wound with soap and water.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Closing and covering</h4>
<ul>
<li>Small, clean cuts can be closed with tape strips.</li>
<li>Leave dirty wounds, bites and punctures <strong>open</strong>, covered with a clean dressing, so they can drain.</li>
<li>Change dressings daily, or when wet or dirty.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Signs of infection</h4>
<ul>
<li>Redness and swelling that spreads</li>
<li>Increasing pain, warmth and pus</li>
<li><strong>Red streaks</strong> moving up from the wound, fever or chills: this is serious. Get to medical care urgently.</li>
</ul>
<p>If infected: open the wound if it was closed, soak in warm clean water several times a day, keep it clean and covered.</p>
<h4>Blisters</h4>
<p>Cover hot spots early with tape. For large blisters, drain from the edge with a clean needle, leave the skin on, and pad around it.</p>
<blockquote>Animal bites can carry rabies. Wash any bite for 15 minutes with soap and water and get medical care as soon as possible.</blockquote>